window.MANO_INDEX=[{"u":"index.html","te":"Home & the DSM-5 map","tn":"","x":"An open atlas of mental disorders · Free to read Mano Atlas A plain-language map of mental disorders, open to anyone learning: DSM-5 diagnostic criteria, teaching diagrams, international examples, and the Nepali context for each. Topics from the CTEVT Psychosocial Counselor curriculum carry a small tag, so counselling students can see at a glance what is examinable. DSM-5 (2013) English Free & open to everyone CTEVT PSC curriculum Beyond curriculum What the two tags mean: every chapter and card carries one. CTEVT PSC curriculum marks content that is on the official Psychosocial Counselor syllabus and can appear in the exam. Beyond curriculum marks content added for completeness (the rest of the DSM-5, newer research, practical extras): worth knowing, not examinable. Readers who are not students can ignore the tags. Worried about someone? Start with the basics, then the chapter that sounds like them. Every chapter opens with where to get help now. Health or community worker? Psychological first aid, the wellbeing pyramid, and case management are your three pages. PSC student? The crosswalk maps every curriculum task to its chapter; the tags show what is examinable. Recently added 24 Techniques step by step 20 Moving forward: change skills 19 Listening back: reflecting skills 22 The first sessions Overview 5 chapters 02 Roots: philosophy to psychology 03 Fear: the foundation 04 Mental health basics 05 Human development Disorder categories 10 chapters 06 Anxiety disorders 07 OCD & related 08 Depressive disorders 09 Trauma & stressor 10 Conversion & somatic 11 Psychosis 12 Child mental illness 13 Eating disorders 14 Sleep-wake disorders 15 Substance use Counselling practice 15 chapters 16 Wellbeing & intervention 17 Approaches & forms 18 Communication skills 19 Listening back: reflecting skills 20 Moving forward: change skills 21 The counselling process 22 The first sessions 23 Tools & techniques 24 Techniques step by step 25 Suicide: assessment 26 PFA & emergencies 27 Gender & GBV 28 HIV & AIDS counselling 29 Case management 30 Ethics & self-care Reference 4 chapters 31 The rest of the map 32 Culture & the Nepal panorama 33 For PSC students 34 Glossary If you or someone near you is struggling right now: in Nepal, call the National Suicide Prevention Helpline 1166 (Mental Hospital, Lagankhel) or the TUTH mental-health hotline 1660 012 1600 ; in an emergency dial 112 or 100 . This site is for learning: it cannot replace a person who can help. The DSM-5 map, where everything lives DSM-5 organises mental disorders into about twenty chapters, ordered roughly by the age at which they typically first appear. Colour marks the categories this atlas covers in depth; the tag on each cell shows whether it sits inside the CTEVT PSC curriculum. On every disorder page a small chip like 296.2x · F32 sits beside the heading: these are the diagnosis codes, the DSM-5's older ICD-9-CM number first, then the ICD-10 code (the \"F-code\") used in hospital records, referral "},{"u":"roots.html","te":"Roots: philosophy to psychology","tn":"","x":"Roots: from philosophy to psychology Psychology is a young science with a very old past. For more than two thousand years before the first laboratory, the questions of this whole site, what is mind? why do people suffer? how do they heal?, belonged to philosophers, physicians, and priests, in Asia as much as in Greece."},{"u":"roots.html#roots-c1","te":"Roots: philosophy to psychology","tn":"","x":"What is psychology? CTEVT PSC curriculum Psychology is the scientific study of mind and behaviour : of what people think, feel, and do, and why. Its four classic goals: describe behaviour, explain it, predict it, and help change it. It earns the word \"scientific\" by testing claims with evidence, observation, surveys, case studies, and experiments, rather than by authority or tradition. Don't confuse Who they are What they do Psychologist Studied psychology (MA/M.Phil/PhD) Assessment, psychotherapy, testing; no medicines Psychiatrist Medical doctor (MD Psychiatry) Diagnoses and prescribes medicines; your referral point for severe illness Psychosocial counselor Trained counselor (e.g., this 780-hour CTEVT course) Detects, supports, counsels, refers: the community's first line Traditional healer (dhami-jhankri) Community spiritual healer First contact for many families; collaborate, don't compete Goal The question it asks In counselling practice Describe What exactly is happening? Naming the pattern: racing heart, avoidance, sleepless nights, for three weeks Explain Why is it happening? Linking the panic to an over-active alarm system and learned avoidance ({{page:foundation}}) Predict What will happen next? Knowing that exam season, a new stressor, or stopping medicine raises relapse risk Control, or help change What can we do about it? Psychoeducation, exposure, activity scheduling, a safety plan Main branches you will meet: clinical & counselling (this course's home), developmental ({{page:development}}), social, cognitive, biological/neuro ({{page:foundation}}), and community psychology ({{page:wellbeing}}). A worked example: A 20-year-old student named Maya keeps skipping lectures and says she feels \"empty.\" Using the four goals of psychology, her counselor describes her behaviour (describe), links it to a pattern of avoidance (explain), predicts that isolation will worsen it without intervention (predict), and sets up structured engagement activities (help change)."},{"u":"roots.html#roots-c2","te":"Roots: philosophy to psychology","tn":"","x":"The pre-psychology era CTEVT PSC curriculum Era & thinkers The idea Why it still matters to a counselor Ancient healing traditions Distress caused by spirits, gods, karma, or imbalance; treated by ritual, trepanation, exorcism This worldview is not \"the past\": it is the first explanation many Nepali clients still receive Hindu & Buddhist psychology (Upanishads from ~800 BCE; Abhidhamma from ~300 BCE) Systematic maps of mind (man, buddhi, chitta, ahamkara); suffering (dukkha) arises from craving and can be worked with through awareness and discipline Mindfulness-based therapies descend from this line; the Nepali \"man\" (heart-mind) you counsel through is part of it Ayurveda & Greek humoral medicine (Hippocrates, ~400 BCE; Charaka, ~100 BCE–200 CE) Mental illness as bodily imbalance, three dosha (vata, pitta, kapha) or four humours, not divine punishment The first naturalistic theory of mental illness: disorder as illness, not sin Greek philosophy; Socrates, Plato, Aristotle Reason examines the self (\"know thyself\"); Plato: mind separate from body; Aristotle: soul as the form of the body, memory and association The talking cure begins here: the belief that examining one's own thoughts can change one's life Stoics (Epictetus, ~100 CE) \"People are disturbed not by things, but by the views they take of them\" Quoted almost word-for-word as the founding idea of CBT Descartes (1600s) Mind-body dualism; the body as machine Gave us the mind/body split that conversion disorder and psychosomatic medicine have been repairing ever since Empiricists: Locke, Hume (1600s–1700s) Mind as blank slate (tabula rasa); knowledge from experience and association The seed of behaviourism and of learning-based therapy: what was learned can be relearned Moral treatment; Pinel, Tuke (1790s) Unchaining asylum patients; illness treated with dignity and routine The direct ancestor of psychosocial care, and of the fight against chaining that Nepal still fights"},{"u":"roots.html#roots-c3","te":"Roots: philosophy to psychology","tn":"","x":"Psychology becomes a science: the schools 1879 → In 1879 Wilhelm Wundt opened the first psychology laboratory in Leipzig, Germany: the conventional birthday of scientific psychology. The schools that followed each kept one piece of the truth, and each gave counselling one of its tools: School Founder(s) Core claim Gift to counselling Structuralism Wundt · Titchener Break consciousness into elements by introspection The habit of careful observation Functionalism William James Ask what the mind is for : adaptation \"What is this symptom doing for the client?\" Psychoanalysis Freud Unconscious conflict, childhood, defence mechanisms The psychodynamic approach; the idea that talking heals Behaviourism Pavlov · Watson · Skinner Behaviour is learned: conditioning and reinforcement Exposure therapy; the reinforcement loop of {{section:foundation}} Humanistic Rogers · Maslow People grow toward their potential given empathy, genuineness, unconditional positive regard The core counselling attitude itself: Rogers' conditions ARE basic counselling Cognitive Beck · Ellis (1950s–60s) Thoughts stand between events and feelings CBT: the most-used approach in this curriculum Socio-cultural / biopsychosocial Vygotsky · Engel (1977) Mind develops in culture; illness is biological + psychological + social at once The lens this whole atlas uses, and the reason \"psychosocial\" is in your job title The thread to hold: every school survives inside your practice. When you normalise a body reaction, you are a biologist; when you trace a thought, a cognitivist; when you set an exposure ladder, a behaviourist; when you listen without judgment, a humanist; when you ask what the family and the village believe, you are doing what no laboratory can, and that is the psychosocial part. Key points Psychology is the scientific study of mind and behaviour; it began as philosophy and became a science in Wundt's lab in 1879. Asia's traditions (Ayurveda, Upanishads, Buddhist psychology) named the mind and its suffering long before Europe did. Each school (psychoanalysis, behaviourism, humanistic, cognitive) left a tool you still use in counselling today. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Scientific psychology's conventional birthday is: Freud's first patient Wundt's 1879 Leipzig laboratory Plato's academy ✔ Yes. The first psychology laboratory made mind a subject of experiment. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. \"People are disturbed not by things but by their views of them\" is the seed of: CBT Psychoanalysis Behaviourism ✔ Yes. The Stoic line is quoted as CBT's founding idea. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Who can prescribe medicine? Psychologist Psychosocial counselor Psychiatrist ✔ Yes. A psychiatrist is a medical doctor; that is your referral point for severe illness. Not quite; have another look. The answer i"},{"u":"foundation.html","te":"Fear: the foundation","tn":"","x":"Fear: the foundation under all of it If you have felt a panic attack or watched someone avoid what they fear, this page is for you. Fear is adaptive; the amygdala's alarm keeps you alive. Anxiety disorders are fear that has become untethered from real danger; the OCD spectrum is what happens when a person finds a behaviour that switches the fear off, and the relief reinforces the behaviour. Learn these two diagrams once and you can explain eight disorders with them. Fight-or-flight. The same body responses that save you from a real threat become the \"symptoms\" a client brings to you, palpitations, tingling, breathlessness, when the alarm fires without danger. The negative reinforcement loop. Phobic avoidance, compulsive washing, reassurance-seeking, mirror-checking in BDD, even bingeing after restriction: the mechanism is one and the same. Treatment (exposure) works by breaking the loop at the safety-behaviour link. Riya avoids dogs after a bite; each avoidance brings relief that makes the fear grow. This is the loop in one life. Key points Fear is the body's alarm; it is useful when danger is real and a problem when it fires without danger. Avoidance brings relief, and relief teaches the brain to avoid again: this loop keeps anxiety alive. Facing the feared thing in small steps, with support, is how the loop is broken. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Palpitations, tingling and breathlessness in panic are: Signs of heart disease Fight-or-flight firing without danger Weak willpower ✔ Yes. The alarm is real and harmless; that one sentence is the core psychoeducation. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. A compulsion grows stronger because: Relief after it reinforces it It is genetic Clients enjoy it ✔ Yes. Negative reinforcement: relief teaches the brain to repeat the behaviour. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Exposure therapy works by breaking the loop at: The trigger The safety behaviour The memory ✔ Yes. Stay with the fear without escaping and the alarm relearns. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Learn more Pick one, not all: these are chosen to be light, not exhaustive. Book chapter Introduction to Psychology: \"Emotion & Stress\" chapter : The fight-or-flight physiology behind every diagram on this page. Video · 19 min Sleep is your superpower (Matt Walker, TED) : Why the fear system needs sleep: bridges this page to the Sleep page."},{"u":"basics.html","te":"Mental health basics","tn":"","x":"Mental health & mental illness: the basics Before any disorder can be named, three questions have to be answered: what is mental health, where is the line between normal and abnormal, and what causes the line to be crossed. This is the CTEVT curriculum's opening block (M2 · Mental Health · Tasks 1–2)."},{"u":"basics.html#basics-c1","te":"Mental health basics","tn":"","x":"Definitions, and the normal–abnormal line CTEVT PSC curriculum Mental health (WHO/your class notes): a state of psychological, emotional, and social well-being in which a person realises their own potential, copes with the normal stresses of life, works productively, and contributes to their community. Mental illness : a clinically significant disturbance in cognition, emotional regulation, or behaviour that causes distress or impairment in functioning. Health is not merely the absence of illness: a person can lack a diagnosis and still be languishing. Where is the \"abnormal\" line? No single test decides it. Clinicians triangulate with the 4 D's : Deviance : far outside cultural norms Distress : it hurts Dysfunction : it breaks daily functioning (work, school, relationships, self-care) Danger : risk to self or others Culture sits inside every D: hearing a dead relative's voice during mourning is deviant in Berlin and expected in Bhaktapur. Frequency, intensity, and duration turn an experience into a symptom. Myth Fact Mental health problems come from personal weakness or weak willpower They result from interacting biological, psychological, and environmental factors Counselling is only for \"severe\" or \"mad\" cases Counselling helps with life transitions, stress, grief, trauma, and everyday emotional regulation People with mental health conditions cannot work With support, most work productively and thrive Children do not get mental health problems Distress can emerge at any age; early intervention improves lifelong outcomes Mental illness is a spirit's curse or the fruit of karma It is a health condition; respectful bridge-building with traditional explanations helps families seek care sooner"},{"u":"basics.html#basics-c2","te":"Mental health basics","tn":"","x":"What causes mental illness: two models to memorise CTEVT PSC curriculum 1 · The biopsychosocial model (Engel, 1977). Every case has three strands braided together: Biological (genes, brain chemistry, physical illness, substances) Psychological (coping style, thought patterns, self-concept, learning history) Social (poverty, family conflict, displacement, discrimination, support networks) The curriculum's own list (biological, psychosocial, environmental) is this model in different words. 2 · The diathesis-stress model. A disorder appears when an underlying vulnerability (diathesis: genetic loading, early adversity, temperament) meets enough stress (loss, migration, exam pressure, disaster). High vulnerability needs little stress; low vulnerability needs a lot. This is why one sibling breaks after an earthquake and another does not, and why \"why now?\" is one of assessment's most powerful questions. Domain Risk factors Protective factors Individual Genetic predisposition, chronic illness, low emotional regulation Problem-solving capacity, resilience, physical health Social Family conflict, domestic violence, isolation, loss Strong social support, healthy family communication Structural Poverty, discrimination, displacement, no access to care Economic stability, safe environment, social inclusion Test yourself: three questions 1) Name the 4 D's and give a Nepali example of each. 2) A woman hears her late husband's voice during the 13-day mourning ritual, which D's are absent, and why does that matter? 3) Use diathesis-stress to explain why two survivors of the same landslide can have opposite outcomes. The stress-performance curve (Yerkes-Dodson, 1908). Some arousal helps: a student with zero exam stress does not study. Mental-health work is not removing stress but keeping people near the middle, and catching them when life pins them to the right edge for months."},{"u":"basics.html#basics-c3","te":"Mental health basics","tn":"","x":"Keeping a mind well: one thinking skill and ten habits CTEVT PSC curriculum Cognitive restructuring is the skill of catching a thought, checking it, and replacing it with a fairer one. The two distortions the class notes name (two of the five listed on {{page:approaches}}): catastrophising (\"I failed one paper, my life is finished\") and black-and-white thinking (\"if I am not the best I am nothing\"). The check is three questions: what is the evidence, what would I tell a friend, what is the most likely outcome. It pairs with the body skills on {{page:tools}} (breath, muscle relaxation, 5-4-3-2-1 grounding) and with behavioural activation on {{page:depression}}. Habit Why it holds a mind up Sleep on a schedule, 7 to 9 hours Sleep is when the brain files emotion and resets stress hormones ({{page:sleep}}) Move every day Walking, farm work, yoga: movement lifts mood through the body's own chemistry A daily calming practice Slow breathing or muscle relaxation keeps the alarm system from idling high Keep people close Belonging is the strongest single buffer against distress Boundaries Being able to say no protects time, energy and self-respect Eat and drink regularly Stable blood sugar and water; go easy on caffeine and alcohol Face problems, don't dodge them Self-compassion and problem-solving beat avoidance, which grows anxiety Limit screens and news Especially before bed: less overload, less rumination Do something that matters Hobbies, creativity, helping: purpose and a sense of mastery Ask for help early Distress caught early is easier to turn; that is what this course exists for Key points Mental health is more than the absence of illness: it is coping, working, relating and contributing. The normal-abnormal line is drawn by distress, dysfunction, duration and danger, never by being different. Illness comes from biology, mind and social world together (biopsychosocial), often as a vulnerability meeting stress. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Which is NOT one of the 4 D's? Distress Diagnosis Dysfunction ✔ Yes. The four are Deviance, Distress, Dysfunction, Danger. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Diathesis-stress says a disorder appears when: Stress alone is enough Vulnerability meets enough stress Genes alone decide ✔ Yes. That is why the same landslide breaks one person and not another. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Hearing a dead relative's voice during the mourning period in Nepal is: Psychosis Usually culturally normal grief Always an emergency ✔ Yes. Culture sits inside every D; context decides what is deviant. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Learn more Pick one, not all: these are chosen to be light, not exhaustive. Video series Crash Course Psychology #1–2 : what psychology is and how it became a science. Paper Engel (1977), \"The ne"},{"u":"development.html","te":"Human development","tn":"","x":"Human development & human needs If you have ever wondered whether you are on track, or worried you fell behind, this page is for you. A counselor reads every client against a map of what is normal for that age and stage . The curriculum asks for three things: the determinants of development, Maslow's hierarchy of needs, and Erikson's eight psychosocial stages."},{"u":"development.html#development-c1","te":"Human development","tn":"","x":"Determinants of development · Maslow's hierarchy CTEVT PSC curriculum Development is driven by four interacting determinants: biological (genes, nutrition, health), cognitive (thinking processes maturing over time), socio-cultural (family, caste/ethnic context, norms, schooling), and environmental (poverty or security, village or city, disaster exposure). Human needs follow a rough order: Maslow's insight is that counselling a hungry, unsafe person about self-esteem skips the floors the building stands on. Maslow's hierarchy. In psychosocial work the bottom two floors are often the intervention: linking a flood-displaced family to shelter and cash support IS mental health care."},{"u":"development.html#development-c2","te":"Human development","tn":"","x":"Erikson's eight psychosocial stages CTEVT PSC curriculum Each stage is a crisis between two poles; resolving it grows a strength (\"virtue\"), failing it leaves a hazard the counselor will meet years later. Ages are approximate. Stage · age Crisis Virtue If it fails: what walks into your room 1 · 0–1½ Trust vs mistrust Hope Difficulty trusting anyone: including the counselor 2 · 1½–3 Autonomy vs shame & doubt Will Chronic shame, indecision, over-control 3 · 3–5 Initiative vs guilt Purpose Guilt at wanting anything for oneself 4 · 6–12 Industry vs inferiority Competence \"I am not capable\": school failure identity 5 · 12–18 Identity vs role confusion Fidelity Drift, peer-pressure vulnerability, identity crises 6 · 18–40 Intimacy vs isolation Love Loneliness, failed closeness, even inside marriage 7 · 40–65 Generativity vs stagnation Care \"What is my life for?\": midlife emptiness 8 · 65+ Integrity vs despair Wisdom Bitterness and despair when looking back Amina, 4, wants to help her mother cook but is told to sit still. She feels guilty for wanting to contribute. This is Initiative vs. Guilt; the virtue at stake is Purpose. Test yourself Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong answer is part of learning, and you can try again. 1) Which Erikson stage is a school-refusing 8-year-old negotiating, and which virtue is at stake? ✔ Yes. Identity vs role confusion is stage 5, and the virtue at stake is Fidelity. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in the Erikson table above. 2) A father abroad for 12 of his son's 15 years: trace the possible impact through stages 1, 4 and 5. ✔ Yes. Stage 1 brings trust difficulties; stage 4 brings an \"I am not capable\" identity; stage 5 brings drift and peer-pressure vulnerability. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in the Erikson table above. 3) Why does Maslow explain the failure of \"just counsel her\" for a woman in an unsafe marriage? ✔ Yes. Maslow's hierarchy: counselling about self-esteem skips the safety floors the building stands on. Safety must be addressed first. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in the Maslow figure above. Key points Development is shaped by biology, thinking, culture and environment acting together. Maslow: basic needs (food, safety) come before self-esteem; sometimes shelter and cash are the counselling. Erikson: each age has a task (trust, autonomy, identity, intimacy...); read the client against their stage. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Which Erikson stage is a school-refusing 8-year-old negotiating, and which virtue is at stake? Industry vs inferiority Identity vs role confusion Autonomy vs shame ✔ Yes. Identity vs role confusion is stage 5, and the virtue at stake is Fidelity. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Why does Maslow explain the failure of \"just counse"},{"u":"anxiety.html","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Anxiety disorders The most common family of mental disorders worldwide. In the CTEVT PSC curriculum this is the first named category (\"Anxiety\", M2 · Sub-module 2 · Task 3)."},{"u":"anxiety.html#anxiety-c1","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Specific Phobia 300.29 · F40.2xx CTEVT PSC curriculum Marked, excessive fear of a specific object or situation, out of proportion to the real danger. In plain words: Being far more afraid of one thing (dogs, heights, blood, flying) than the danger deserves, and arranging life to avoid it. DSM-5 Criteria Marked fear or anxiety about a specific object or situation (flying, heights, animals, injections, blood…). In children: crying, tantrums, freezing, clinging. The phobic object or situation almost always provokes immediate fear. It is actively avoided, or endured with intense fear. The fear is out of proportion to the actual danger and to the sociocultural context. Duration 6 months or more. Clinically significant distress or impairment in functioning. Not better explained by another disorder (panic, OCD, separation anxiety, PTSD, social anxiety). Five specifiers by stimulus: Animal Natural environment Blood-injection-injury (the only one with fainting; vasovagal) Situational Other International One of the most common mental disorders worldwide (US 12-month prevalence ≈ 7–9%), typically beginning in childhood (median onset ~7–11 years). Fear content is universal, but which fears dominate varies by ecology: snake and animal phobias where those threats are real, flying and injection phobias in industrialised settings. Nepal No national prevalence figure exists: never invent one. Presentations often arrive as body complaints (\"mutu dhukdhuk\", dizziness) rather than \"fear\". Distinguish a phobia from realistic caution: fearing a landslide path in monsoon is adaptive; refusing all travel year-round is not. Fear following a spirit-related interpretation (e.g., fear of a place after \"saato gaeko\": soul loss/fright) needs the cultural meaning explored before any label. A worked example: A 19-year-old named Arun avoids every bus ride because he fears losing control and screaming. His counselor distinguishes this from a phobia of travel itself, notes that the avoidance has spread from buses to leaving home, and explains that the panic curve figure shows what actually happens: the body cannot hold the alarm forever."},{"u":"anxiety.html#anxiety-c2","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Social Anxiety Disorder (Social Phobia) 300.23 · F40.10 CTEVT PSC curriculum Marked fear of social situations where one may be scrutinised: driven by fear of negative evaluation. In plain words: Dreading being looked at or judged (speaking, eating, meeting people), so that these situations are avoided or endured in fear. DSM-5 Criteria Marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations with possible scrutiny by others: conversation, being observed, performing. In children it must occur with peers, not only adults. Fear of acting in a way, or showing anxiety symptoms, that will be negatively evaluated: humiliation, embarrassment, rejection, or offending others. The situations almost always provoke fear or anxiety. They are avoided or endured with intense fear. The fear is out of proportion to the actual threat and sociocultural context. Duration 6 months or more. Clinically significant distress or impairment. Not attributable to a substance or medical condition. Not better explained by another mental disorder. If a medical condition (e.g., disfigurement, stuttering) is present, the fear is unrelated or excessive. Specifier: performance only (public speaking/performing). International In Japan and Korea, the related cultural concept taijin kyofusho reverses the Western fear: the sufferer fears making others uncomfortable; with one's blushing, gaze, or body odour. DSM-5 lists it as a cultural concept of distress; it shows how the same mechanism (fear of social evaluation) is shaped by a collectivist frame. Nepal Think of ijjat (इज्जत): family honour and face. Fear of shaming the family can amplify social fears, and \"log le ke bhanchan?\" (what will people say?) is a common worry frame. In classrooms, a student who never speaks, avoids the front bench, and misses presentations may be labelled \"lajalu\" (shy) for years before anyone considers a treatable disorder. Differentiate culturally normal deference (not speaking before elders) from impairing fear."},{"u":"anxiety.html#anxiety-c3","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Separation Anxiety Disorder 309.21 · F93.0 CTEVT PSC curriculum Developmentally inappropriate, excessive fear about separation from attachment figures. In plain words: A child (or adult) who cannot bear being apart from the people they are attached to, far beyond what is normal for their age. DSM-5 Criteria Excessive fear about separation from attachment figures, shown by at least 3 of 8 : Recurrent distress when separation occurs or is anticipated Persistent worry about losing attachment figures (illness, injury, death) Worry that an event (getting lost, kidnapped) will cause separation Reluctance or refusal to go out or to school Fear of being alone without attachment figures Reluctance to sleep away from home or without the figure nearby Repeated nightmares with separation themes Physical symptoms (headache, stomachache, nausea) at separation Duration: at least 4 weeks in children and adolescents; typically 6 months or more in adults. Clinically significant distress or impairment. Not better explained by another disorder (autism, psychosis, agoraphobia, GAD, illness anxiety). International DSM-5's key change: it is no longer only a childhood disorder; adult onset is recognised, often around leaving home, migration, or the birth of a child. The most common anxiety disorder in children under 12. Nepal Labour migration is the defining Nepali context: millions of households have a parent abroad. A child refusing school with daily stomachaches after a father leaves for the Gulf is a classic presentation, and the family may read it as physical illness or school laziness. Joint-family co-sleeping is normal; measure \"developmentally inappropriate\" against Nepali norms, not Western sleeping-alone norms. (DACUM anchor: \"assess the childhood history; attachments\".)"},{"u":"anxiety.html#anxiety-c4","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) 300.02 · F41.1 CTEVT PSC curriculum Excessive, hard-to-control worry about many things at once: the \"worry about everything\" disorder. In plain words: Worrying about many things most days for months, unable to switch it off, with a tense, tired, sleepless body. DSM-5 Criteria Excessive anxiety and worry, more days than not for at least 6 months , about a number of events or activities (work, school, family, health). The person finds the worry difficult to control. At least 3 of 6 symptoms (1 suffices in children): restlessness/keyed-up · easily fatigued · difficulty concentrating or mind going blank · irritability · muscle tension · sleep disturbance. Clinically significant distress or impairment. Not attributable to a substance or medical condition (e.g., hyperthyroidism). Not better explained by another disorder (panic, social anxiety, OCD, PTSD, illness anxiety, anorexia). International Twice as common in women; in primary care worldwide it usually presents as tiredness, aches, and poor sleep rather than \"worry\". High-income settings show later onset (often 30s) than most anxiety disorders. Nepal The classic Nepali GAD presentation is somatic: \"jiu dukhcha, nindra lagdaina, man aattincha\" (body aches, no sleep, the heart-mind panics). Common worry themes: debt and loans, a son abroad, land disputes, daughters' marriages, crop failure. Screen for thyroid disease and anaemia before concluding GAD: both are common and mimic it. Screening tool: GAD-7 (a validated Nepali version exists) Time frame: the past two weeks. Answers: 0 not at all 1 several days 2 more than half the days 3 nearly every day Seven items: Feeling nervous or anxious Unable to stop worrying Worrying about many things Trouble relaxing Restlessness Irritability Fear that something awful may happen The Nepali adaptation speaks the language of the heart-mind. Score: 0 to 21; roughly 5 mild, 10 moderate, 15 severe. Ten or more deserves fuller assessment. A screen suggests; it never diagnoses."},{"u":"anxiety.html#anxiety-c5","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Panic Disorder 300.01 · F41.0 CTEVT PSC curriculum Recurrent, unexpected surges of intense fear peaking within minutes: then a month of fearing the next one. In plain words: Sudden waves of terror with a pounding heart and breathlessness that feel like dying, then a month of fearing the next one. DSM-5 Criteria Recurrent unexpected panic attacks : an abrupt surge of intense fear peaking within minutes, with 4 or more of 13 symptoms: Body: palpitations · sweating · trembling · shortness of breath · choking feeling · chest pain · nausea · dizziness · chills or heat · numbness or tingling Altered experience: the world or oneself feels unreal (derealisation, depersonalisation) Feared outcomes: losing control or \"going crazy\" · dying At least 1 month after an attack of one or both: persistent worry about further attacks or their consequences; or a significant maladaptive change in behaviour (avoiding exercise, unfamiliar places). Not attributable to a substance or medical condition (hyperthyroidism, cardiopulmonary disease). Not better explained by another disorder (the attacks are not cued only by social situations, phobic objects, obsessions, trauma reminders, or separation). A panic attack is a symptom, not a diagnosis: it can ride on any disorder (\"with panic attacks\" specifier). Panic disorder requires unexpected attacks plus the month of aftermath. International DSM-5 names culture-specific panic idioms: ataque de nervios (Latin America; shouting, crying, trembling after family stress) and khyâl cap (\"wind attacks\", Cambodia: panic attributed to wind rising in the body). Same physiology, different explanatory story. Nepal Clients usually reach the emergency room or a cardiologist first: chest pain and breathlessness read as a heart attack. \"Mutu kamjor bhayo\" (my heart has become weak) is a common self-diagnosis. Psychoeducation with the fight-or-flight body map above is often the single most effective first intervention: the symptoms are real, dangerous-feeling, and, once a doctor has ruled out a heart or lung cause, harmless. The panic curve. Panic feels endless from inside; physiologically it peaks within about ten minutes and must come down. Riding one attack out, without escaping, is how the brain relearns safety; escaping at the peak is how agoraphobia begins."},{"u":"anxiety.html#anxiety-c6","te":"Anxiety disorders","tn":"","x":"Agoraphobia 300.22 · F40.00 CTEVT PSC curriculum Fear of situations where escape might be difficult or help unavailable if panic-like symptoms strike. In plain words: Avoiding crowds, buses, open or closed places because escape or help might not be possible if panic strikes. DSM-5 Criteria Marked fear about 2 or more of 5 situations: public transport · open spaces (markets, bridges) · enclosed places (shops, cinemas) · standing in line or being in a crowd · being outside the home alone. Feared because escape would be difficult or help unavailable if panic-like or embarrassing symptoms developed. The situations almost always provoke fear. They are avoided, require a companion, or are endured with intense fear. Fear out of proportion to actual danger. 6 months or more. Clinically significant distress or impairment. If a medical condition is present, the fear is clearly excessive. Not better explained by another disorder. Diagnosed independently of panic disorder: a person can have both. Nepal · International Internationally the picture is the person housebound for years. In Nepal, watch for it hidden behind role explanations: a daughter-in-law who \"prefers\" never to leave the house alone, or an elder who stopped attending the bazaar after one dizzy spell. Crowded microbuses and jatras are common avoided situations. Also in this chapter: Selective Mutism (312.23/F94.0): a child speaks normally at home but consistently fails to speak in specific social situations (school) for ≥1 month, interfering with education; not explained by lack of language knowledge. Common first referral from schools. A skill to try · two minutes Slow breathing, 4 in · 6 out. Breathe in through the nose for a count of 4, out slowly for 6. The longer out-breath tells the fight-or-flight alarm to stand down. Two minutes, shoulders loose, feet on the floor. Free illustrated guide with more skills: WHO, Doing What Matters in Times of Stress . How a counselor works with avoidance over several sessions (ACT) is on {{page:techniques}}. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. Key points Anxiety disorders are fear out of proportion to real danger, lasting 6 months or more (1 month for panic). Each has a focus: an object (phobia), being watched (social), separation, everything (GAD), the body's alarm (panic), escape (agoraphobia). Psychoeducation and gradual exposure are the core treatments; in Nepal anxiety usually arrives as body complaints first. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Minimum duration for specific phobia and social anxiety: 2 weeks 6 months 2 years ✔ Yes. Six months separates a passing fear from a disorder. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Panic disorder requires unexpected attacks plus: A month of worry or changed behaviour Daily attacks A heart problem ✔ Yes. The aftermath, fearing the n"},{"u":"ocd.html","te":"OCD & related","tn":"","x":"Obsessive-compulsive & related disorders DSM-5 moved OCD out of the anxiety chapter into its own family: disorders driven by repetitive behaviour that relieves distress. (In Nepali PSC training OCD is still taught within the anxiety block; answer exams accordingly.)"},{"u":"ocd.html#ocd-c1","te":"OCD & related","tn":"","x":"Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) 300.3 · F42 CTEVT PSC curriculum Unwanted intrusive thoughts (obsessions) and the rituals performed to neutralise them (compulsions). In plain words: Unwanted thoughts that keep coming back (germs, harm, doubt), and rituals (washing, checking, counting) done to quiet them. DSM-5 Criteria Presence of obsessions, compulsions, or both. Obsessions: recurrent, persistent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges, or images causing anxiety; the person tries to ignore, suppress, or neutralise them with another thought or action. Compulsions: repetitive behaviours (washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, praying, repeating words) performed in response to an obsession or rigid rules, aimed at reducing distress or preventing a dreaded event, but not realistically connected to it, or clearly excessive. Time-consuming (more than 1 hour/day ) or causing clinically significant distress or impairment. Not attributable to a substance or medical condition. Not better explained by another disorder (GAD worry, BDD appearance-preoccupation, hoarding, eating-disorder food rituals…). Specifiers: Insight: good/fair Insight: poor Insight: absent-delusional Tic-related Common themes: Contamination Doubt/harm Symmetry Forbidden (religious/sexual) thoughts International Lifetime prevalence ~1–2% worldwide with a remarkably similar picture across countries; content follows culture: religious scrupulosity dominates in devout communities (fear of blasphemy in one faith, ritual-purity doubts in another), contamination in germ-aware urban settings. Nepal Culture supplies ready-made ritual channels: chokho/jutho (pure/polluted) rules, bathing after contact, puja repetition. The diagnostic line is function, not content: ritual purity observed like one's community is culture; washing until the skin breaks, or repeating puja for hours out of dread, is disorder. No national prevalence figure exists. Families often reach a dhami-jhankri first; engage respectfully rather than dismissing."},{"u":"ocd.html#ocd-c2","te":"OCD & related","tn":"","x":"Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD) 300.7 · F45.22 Beyond curriculum Preoccupation with perceived appearance defects that others cannot see or find slight: formerly \"dysmorphophobia\". In plain words: Being consumed by a flaw in one's appearance that others barely see, with hours spent checking, hiding or fixing it. DSM-5 Criteria Preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance not observable, or appearing slight, to others. At some point, repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, excessive grooming, skin picking, reassurance seeking) or mental acts (comparing one's appearance with others') in response to the concerns. Clinically significant distress or impairment. Not better explained by an eating disorder (weight/fat concern in a person meeting eating-disorder criteria is not BDD). Specifiers: muscle dysmorphia (mostly men); insight; one-third or more hold delusional conviction. High suicide risk. Cosmetic treatment almost never helps and often worsens it. International ~2.4% of US adults; 9–15% of dermatology patients. Shubo-kyofu in Japan (fear of a deformed body) is the recognised cultural variant. Onset is adolescent: two-thirds before 18. Nepal Expect it in beauty parlours, dermatology OPDs and among urban youth on Instagram/TikTok, not in counselling rooms. Fairness-cream culture and marriage-market appearance pressure give the preoccupation local content (skin tone, nose shape). Screen when a young client repeatedly seeks skin or cosmetic fixes for a defect you cannot see."},{"u":"ocd.html#ocd-c3","te":"OCD & related","tn":"","x":"The rest of the OCD family; at a glance Beyond curriculum Disorder Code Core feature Hoarding disorder 300.3 · F42 Persistent difficulty discarding possessions regardless of value; accumulation clutters living areas Trichotillomania 312.39 · F63.3 Recurrent hair pulling with hair loss; repeated attempts to stop Excoriation disorder 698.4 · L98.1 Recurrent skin picking causing lesions; repeated attempts to stop The OCD loop. The ritual is the trap: it works for a minute and teaches the brain to demand it again. Treatment (ERP) breaks the loop at the compulsion, which is why reassurance and helping with rituals, however kind, keep it turning. A skill to try · two minutes Urge surfing. When the urge to check or wash rises, name it (\"the alarm again\"), set a five-minute delay, and watch the urge like a wave. Often it crests and passes; sometimes it lingers, and the practice is the same: not obeying it. Every ridden wave weakens the loop from the diagram on {{page:foundation}}. Full treatment belongs with a professional; this is practice for small waves. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. A worked example: A 24-year-old named Raj has intrusive thoughts that his hands are contaminated. He washes them for 40 minutes each time, missing work. His therapist names the obsession and compulsion, explains that the ritual feeds the loop shown in the figure above, and starts exposure with response prevention: he touches a doorknob and delays washing by five minutes. Key points OCD is obsessions (unwanted thoughts) plus compulsions (rituals that relieve them), taking more than an hour a day. The ritual works for a minute and feeds the loop for years; that is why reassurance does not help. Exposure with response prevention (ERP) is the treatment; body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding and hair-pulling are the same family. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. A compulsion is best described as: A pleasurable habit A repetitive act done to reduce obsession anxiety A psychotic symptom ✔ Yes. Wash, check, count, pray: the act answers the intrusive thought. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. OCD's time criterion: More than 1 hour a day More than 15 minutes All day ✔ Yes. Over an hour daily, or significant distress or impairment. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. In BDD, cosmetic treatment usually: Cures it Does not help and often worsens it Is the first-line treatment ✔ Yes. The problem is the preoccupation, not the face; that is why referral matters. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Learn more Pick one, not all: these are chosen to be light, not exhaustive. Video · 4 min Debunking the myths of OCD (TED-Ed) : Four minutes that fix the \"I'm so OCD\" misunderstanding. Book Brain Lock (Jeffrey Schwartz) : The four-step self-treatment method: "},{"u":"depression.html","te":"Depressive disorders","tn":"","x":"Depressive disorders The curriculum's second named type of mental illness, and a leading cause of disability worldwide. If you or someone you love is thinking about death or suicide: call 1166 or 1660 012 1600 now, or 112 / 100 in an emergency. Asking about it directly is safe, and it helps."},{"u":"depression.html#depression-c1","te":"Depressive disorders","tn":"","x":"Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) 296.2x/3x · F32–F33 CTEVT PSC curriculum At least two weeks of depressed mood or loss of pleasure, plus enough companion symptoms to reach five. In plain words: Two weeks or more of feeling low or empty, or losing interest in everything, with sleep, appetite, energy and hope all worse. DSM-5 Criteria 5 or more of the 9 symptoms below, during the same 2-week period, a change from before, and at least one is (1) depressed mood or (2) anhedonia . Clinically significant distress or impairment. Not attributable to a substance or medical condition. Not better explained by a schizophrenia-spectrum disorder. There has never been a manic or hypomanic episode (if there has, the diagnosis is bipolar disorder). 1 · core Depressed mood most of the day, nearly every day (in children: irritability) 2 · core Markedly diminished interest or pleasure in almost all activities (anhedonia) 3 Significant weight/appetite change (loss or gain) 4 Insomnia or hypersomnia nearly every day 5 Psychomotor agitation or retardation, observable by others 6 Fatigue or loss of energy 7 Feelings of worthlessness or excessive, inappropriate guilt 8 Diminished ability to think or concentrate; indecisiveness 9 Recurrent thoughts of death, suicidal ideation, plan, or attempt Key specifiers: with anxious distress · with melancholic features · with psychotic features · with peripartum onset (pregnancy or within 4 weeks of delivery: \"postpartum depression\") · with seasonal pattern. Severity: mild / moderate / severe. International WHO ranks depression among the leading causes of disability worldwide: over 330 million people affected. Women are affected about 1.5 times as often as men. The \"with peripartum onset\" specifier matters everywhere: postpartum depression affects more than 1 in 10 mothers globally and is routinely missed. Nepal The Nepali client says \"man dukhcha\", \"man bhari bhayo\", \"jiu galyo\": the heart-mind hurts, the body has wilted, not \"I am depressed\". Suicide is the sharpest edge: studies since the 2008/09 maternal mortality review have repeatedly flagged suicide as a leading cause of death among Nepali women of reproductive age. Every depression assessment includes a direct, calm suicide question: asking does not plant the idea. Watch new mothers especially: family attention goes to the baby, and the mother's withdrawal is read as tiredness."},{"u":"depression.html#depression-c2","te":"Depressive disorders","tn":"","x":"Persistent Depressive Disorder · and the bipolar boundary 300.4 · F34.1 CTEVT PSC curriculum Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia): depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for 2 years or more (1 year in children or adolescents, where mood can be irritable), with 2+ of the following. Symptoms include: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and hopelessness. Never symptom-free for more than 2 months. The client who says \"I have been like this my whole life.\" A manic episode (at least one week, or any duration if hospitalised) makes it Bipolar I ; a milder hypomanic episode (at least four days, no marked impairment) plus major depression makes it Bipolar II . Antidepressants alone can flip an unrecognised bipolar client into mania: this question is a safety question, and a referral trigger. The depression spiral. Each link feels reasonable from inside (\"I will go out when I feel better\"), yet each turn tightens the next. Behavioural activation attacks the easiest link to move: the activity, not the mood; the steps a counselor uses are on {{page:techniques}}. A skill to try · two minutes One tiny scheduled action. Pick something five minutes small: wash one cup, step onto the balcony, message one person. Give it a time (\"after tea\") and do it whether or not the mood agrees; the spiral diagram above shows why action comes first. Tonight, name one thing that went okay. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. A worked example: A 32-year-old named Sita, a mother of two, has lost interest in playing with her children and feels most drained in the afternoons. Her counselor confirms that low mood, poor sleep, and loss of pleasure have lasted over three weeks, meets the five-symptom threshold, and rules out bipolar disorder before beginning treatment. Key points Depression is at least two weeks of low mood or lost pleasure plus enough other symptoms to reach five, with daily life affected. Always ask about thoughts of death; asking never plants the idea, and it opens the door to help. Behavioural activation (small planned activity) and talking treatments work; in Nepal it often shows as aches, tiredness and 'man dukhcha'. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. MDD requires how many of the nine symptoms, for how long? 5 in 2 weeks, including a core one 3 in 1 week All 9 in a month ✔ Yes. Five of nine, two weeks, and one must be low mood or anhedonia. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Before calling it depression, always ask about: Appetite only Past manic or hypomanic episodes Childhood grades ✔ Yes. A missed manic episode changes the diagnosis to bipolar and changes the treatment. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Asking a client directly about suicide: Plant"},{"u":"trauma.html","te":"Trauma & stressor","tn":"","x":"Trauma- and stressor-related disorders The DSM-5 chapter built around an external event: no traumatic or stressful event, no diagnosis. A CTEVT PSC curriculum topic (trauma, PFA, IASC)."},{"u":"trauma.html#trauma-c1","te":"Trauma & stressor","tn":"","x":"Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) 309.81 · F43.10 CTEVT PSC curriculum After exposure to death, serious injury, or sexual violence: one month or more of intrusion, avoidance, negative mood, and hyperarousal. In plain words: After a terrible event, the mind keeps replaying it, avoids reminders, turns dark, and stays on high alert for more than a month. First, the reassurance: most people who live through a terrible event do not develop PTSD. Fear, poor sleep, jumpiness and replaying the event in the first weeks are the mind's normal response. PTSD is the name for when this does not settle after a month and takes over daily life. DSM-5 Criteria (adults) Exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence: directly · witnessing · learning it happened to a close one (violent/accidental) · repeated exposure to aversive details (first responders). Media exposure alone does not count unless work-related. ≥1 intrusion symptom (see cluster B below). ≥1 avoidance symptom (cluster C). ≥2 negative alterations in cognitions and mood (cluster D). ≥2 arousal and reactivity changes (cluster E). Duration of B–E more than 1 month . Clinically significant distress or impairment. Not attributable to a substance or medical condition. B · Intrusion need at least 1 of 5 Intrusive memories Trauma nightmares Flashbacks Distress at reminders Physiological reactions to cues C · Avoidance need at least 1 of 2 Avoiding memories, thoughts, feelings Avoiding external reminders (people, places, activities) D · Negative cognition & mood need at least 2 of 7 Can't recall key parts Negative beliefs (\"world is dangerous\") Distorted self/other blame Persistent fear, anger, shame Diminished interest Detachment from others Can't feel positive emotions E · Arousal & reactivity need at least 2 of 6 Irritability, anger outbursts Reckless, self-destructive behaviour Hypervigilance Exaggerated startle Poor concentration Sleep disturbance Specifiers: with dissociative symptoms (depersonalisation/derealisation) · with delayed expression (full criteria only after 6 months). Acute Stress Disorder (308.3/F43.0) is a closely related picture (9 or more of 14 symptoms) lasting 3 days to 1 month; Adjustment Disorder (F43.2x) is distress out of proportion to a non-traumatic stressor, within 3 months of it. International First systematised after the Vietnam war (\"shell shock\", \"war neurosis\" before that); today the heaviest burdens are in conflict zones and among refugees and survivors of sexual violence. Most people exposed to trauma do not develop PTSD: resilience is the norm; risk rises with dose, proximity, and interpersonal violence. Nepal Three national layers: the 1996–2006 conflict (disappearances, torture survivors), the 2015 earthquakes, and yearly floods and landslides; plus trafficking survivors and returned migrant workers. Post-earthquake research (e.g., TPO Nepal studies) found distress often expressed through the heart-mind: fear idioms like \"man aatteko\", \""},{"u":"trauma.html#trauma-c2","te":"Trauma & stressor","tn":"","x":"Generational (intergenerational) trauma Beyond curriculum Trauma's effects can pass from one generation to the next. The pain may not have started with you, but healing can start with you. How it transmits, through five channels: Biology and the stress response: long stress changes the body's alarm systems, and those settings shape how children react Learned behaviour: children absorb parents' anger, fear, love, and problem-solving styles Silence and secrecy: hidden painful events still leak their emotional weight into the next generation Relationship patterns: insecure attachment, emotional distance, and low trust repeat across generations Survival behaviours: pleasing everyone, taking all responsibility, suppressing feelings These once kept a family alive; later they become the problem itself. Understanding is not blaming. The point is never to accuse the older generation; it is to recognise the inherited pain, behaviours, and beliefs, and then choose consciously what to carry forward: love, compassion, healthy patterns, truth, connection, hope. Awareness is the first step; change is a decision. Paths that help: counselling and therapy, writing and expression, breathing and meditation practice, support groups and community, self-care, and honest conversation inside the family. Some negative cycles can end with you. Nepal Three Nepali examples of trauma that travels through families: the 1996 to 2006 conflict, whose survivors' children grew up with unspoken disappearances and fear; caste-based humiliation carried across generations as shame and guardedness; and long labour-migration separations. Family silence is common and culturally understandable; the counselor's craft is opening space for the story without forcing it. A worked example: A 28-year-old named Tara, whose father disappeared during the conflict, avoids crowded markets and startles at loud sounds. Her counselor explains that her avoidance and hypervigilance are the mind's protective response, not weakness, and that the window of tolerance diagram shows how grounding can widen her capacity before any trauma-focused work begins. A skill to try · two minutes 5-4-3-2-1 grounding. Feet flat on the floor. Name 5 things you can see, 4 you can touch, 3 you can hear, 2 you can smell, 1 you can taste. This walks the mind back from then to now, back inside the window of tolerance above. Cold water on the face works the same way. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. Key points PTSD needs an actual traumatic event and four symptom groups (re-experiencing, avoidance, negative mood, arousal) for more than a month. Most people recover on their own with safety, support and time; only some develop PTSD. Trauma can travel through families; understanding it is not blaming, and the cycle can end with one person. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again."},{"u":"somatic.html","te":"Conversion & somatic","tn":"","x":"Conversion & somatic symptom disorders The curriculum's third named type, and in Nepal, arguably the most important differential a psychosocial counselor ever makes: conversion versus epilepsy."},{"u":"somatic.html#somatic-c1","te":"Conversion & somatic","tn":"","x":"Conversion Disorder (Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder) 300.11 · F44.x CTEVT PSC curriculum Real neurological symptoms, paralysis, seizures, blindness, mutism, that are incompatible with any neurological disease. The client is not pretending. In plain words: Real symptoms that look neurological (fits, weakness, blindness, losing the voice) without a disease to explain them; the body is speaking. DSM-5 Criteria One or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function. Clinical findings show incompatibility between the symptom and recognised neurological or medical conditions (e.g., a normal EEG recorded during a \"seizure\", though this alone does not exclude every form of epilepsy; tremor that stops when attention shifts). Not better explained by another medical or mental disorder. Clinically significant distress, impairment, or warrants medical evaluation. Symptom types: Weakness or paralysis Abnormal movement Swallowing Speech Attacks or seizures (PNES) Anaesthesia or sensory loss Special sensory (vision, hearing) Mixed DSM-5 dropped the old requirement to prove a psychological stressor, and \"la belle indifférence\" is not diagnostic. Epileptic seizure Conversion (psychogenic non-epileptic) attack Onset Sudden, any time: including sleep, alone Gradual, usually with witnesses present Movements Rhythmic, synchronous jerking; stereotyped Asynchronous thrashing, pelvic thrusting, side-to-side head shaking, arc de cercle Eyes Usually open or deviated Usually closed, resist opening Injury & incontinence Side-of-tongue biting is fairly specific; falls and incontinence occur Also occur; no single sign settles it, video-EEG does Duration Typically 1–2 minutes Often prolonged, waxing-waning, >5 minutes After the attack Confusion, drowsiness (post-ictal) Rapid recovery, may cry; recall variable EEG Epileptiform activity Normal during attack Nepal: including mass conversion This is a signature Nepali presentation: episodes of fainting, jerking, or possession-like states (\"chhopne\") in adolescent girls, often spreading through a school classroom or hostel within hours: mass conversion / mass psychogenic illness , repeatedly reported in Nepali districts. Respond by separating affected students calmly, avoiding dramatic audiences, ruling out real medical causes, and working with the community's spiritual explanation rather than against it. Never announce \"it is all in her mind\": the symptom is real, involuntary, and treatable, and the family's cooperation is your main instrument. Refer first-ever seizures for neurological work-up always. Also in this chapter: Somatic Symptom Disorder (300.82/F45.1): one or more distressing bodily symptoms plus excessive thoughts, anxiety, or time devoted to them, persistent (typically >6 months); the symptom may or may not be medically explained: what is disordered is the response to it. Illness Anxiety Disorder (300.7/F45.21): preoccupation with having a serious illness, with minimal or no somatic symptoms; ch"},{"u":"psychosis.html","te":"Psychosis","tn":"","x":"Schizophrenia spectrum & other psychotic disorders The curriculum's fourth named type. Psychosis = a break with shared reality: delusions, hallucinations, disorganisation. The counselor's role is detection, family psychoeducation, and referral, not solo management. If someone is hearing voices or believing things that frighten them: stay calm, keep them safe, and take them to a hospital psychiatric service, or call 1166 or 1660 012 1600 . This is an illness that treatment helps; it is not a curse or a punishment. A worked example: A 22-year-old named Bishal has started whispering to himself and says his neighbors are plotting against him. His family hides him indoors, fearing the \"paagal\" label His counselor recognizes that hearing culturally sanctioned voices alone is not psychosis, but the decline in functioning and the disorganized speech are flags The counselor makes an urgent referral, keeps Bishal's dignity intact, and begins family psychoeducation about the difference between spiritual experience and illness"},{"u":"psychosis.html#psychosis-c1","te":"Psychosis","tn":"","x":"Schizophrenia 295.90 · F20.9 CTEVT PSC curriculum Positive symptoms: added to experience Delusions : fixed false beliefs (persecution, reference, grandeur, control) Hallucinations : perceptions without stimulus; hearing voices is the classic Disorganised speech : derailment, incoherence, \"word salad\" Grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour Negative symptoms: subtracted from experience Diminished emotional expression : flat affect, monotone Avolition : loss of motivated, purposeful activity Alogia (poverty of speech) · anhedonia · asociality Negative symptoms predict long-term disability more than positive ones DSM-5 Criteria 2 or more of 5 , each present a significant portion of one month (less if treated), and at least one must be (1), (2), or (3): delusions · hallucinations · disorganised speech · grossly disorganised/catatonic behaviour · negative symptoms. Functioning (work, relationships, self-care) markedly below the level before onset. Continuous signs for at least 6 months , including ≥1 month of active symptoms (may include prodromal/residual periods). Schizoaffective disorder and mood disorder with psychotic features ruled out. Not attributable to a substance or medical condition. If autism or a childhood communication disorder is present, prominent delusions or hallucinations ≥1 month are also required. Cousins on the spectrum: Brief psychotic disorder (298.8/F23): ≥1 day, <1 month, full return, often after acute stress (including postpartum). Schizophreniform : schizophrenia picture, 1–6 months. Delusional disorder (297.1/F22): delusions ≥1 month with otherwise preserved functioning. Schizoaffective : schizophrenia criteria plus major mood episodes, with ≥2 weeks of psychosis without mood symptoms. International Lifetime prevalence ~0.3–0.7% in every population studied; onset typically late teens to early 30s, earlier in men. A famous WHO cross-national finding: outcomes were often better in lower-income countries; family involvement and social reintegration appear protective (later re-analyses have contested how general this finding is). Nepal Stigma is the central fact: \"paagal\" labels, hidden family members, and in the worst cases chaining; plus years of dhami-jhankri treatment before any clinical contact. Hearing culturally sanctioned voices (a deceased relative during mourning, deity communication in trance for a jhankri) is not psychosis; the flags are decline in functioning, disorganisation, and distress. First contact is often the counselor's: your jobs are early detection, urgent psychiatric referral, family psychoeducation, and protecting the client's dignity and safety. Two kinds of symptom, two kinds of misunderstanding. Positive symptoms frighten the family; negative symptoms get mistaken for laziness. Psychoeducation that names both is the counselor's most useful hour in a psychosis household. A skill to try · two minutes For families: steady the everyday. Recovery leans on rhythm: regular sleep, meals at usual times"},{"u":"child.html","te":"Child mental illness","tn":"","x":"Child mental illness & neurodevelopmental disorders The curriculum's fifth item (\"Be familiar with child's mental illness\"). Separation anxiety is covered on the Anxiety disorders page; here are the four the CTEVT curriculum and the Mental Health-3 class notes name: intellectual disability, ADHD, autism, and the behaviour disorders."},{"u":"child.html#child-c1","te":"Child mental illness","tn":"","x":"Intellectual Disability (Intellectual Developmental Disorder) 319 · F70–F79 CTEVT PSC curriculum DSM-5 Criteria: all three required Deficits in intellectual functions : reasoning, problem-solving, planning, abstract thinking, judgment, academic learning, learning from experience: confirmed by clinical assessment and standardised testing (IQ roughly ≤ 70). Deficits in adaptive functioning across three domains: conceptual (language, literacy, number, memory), social (empathy, social judgment, friendships, gullibility), practical (self-care, money, safety, work, transport). Onset during the developmental period (childhood or adolescence; the class notes and CTEVT say \"before age 18\"). Severity, mild / moderate / severe / profound, is graded by adaptive functioning and support needed , not IQ alone. Causes: genetic, prenatal, perinatal, and postnatal. ID is a permanent developmental state, not an episodic illness. The counselor's work is family psychoeducation, skill-building with caregivers, disability-card and special-education advocacy, and caregiver support. Aspect Intellectual disability Mental illness Nature Developmental limitation in intelligence & adaptive skills Disturbance of mood, thinking, or behaviour Onset During the developmental period Any point in life Course Permanent state; skills can grow with support Episodes that can remit, relapse, be treated Main response Skill training, environmental support, inclusion Therapy, medication, recovery-oriented care"},{"u":"child.html#child-c2","te":"Child mental illness","tn":"","x":"ADHD · Autism · ODD · Conduct Disorder CTEVT PSC curriculum ADHD: 314.0x · F90.x ≥6 inattention symptoms , and/or ≥6 hyperactivity-impulsivity symptoms , for ≥6 months (≥5 from age 17): Inattention: careless mistakes · can't sustain attention · doesn't seem to listen · fails to finish · disorganised · avoids sustained mental effort · loses things · distractible · forgetful Hyperactivity-impulsivity: fidgets · leaves seat · runs/climbs · can't play quietly · \"driven by a motor\" · talks excessively · blurts answers · can't wait turn · interrupts Several symptoms present before age 12 . Present in 2 or more settings (home and school). Clear interference with functioning; and Not better explained by another disorder. Autism Spectrum Disorder: 299.00 · F84.0 Persistent deficits in social communication and interaction: all 3 : social-emotional reciprocity · nonverbal communication · developing and understanding relationships. Restricted, repetitive behaviour: ≥2 of 4 : stereotyped movements/speech (lining up toys, echolalia) · insistence on sameness and routines · highly restricted, intense interests · sensory hyper- or hypo-reactivity. Present in the early developmental period. Clinically significant impairment. Not better explained by intellectual disability alone. Oppositional Defiant Disorder (313.81/F91.3): ≥6 months of angry or irritable mood, argumentative-defiant behaviour toward authority, or vindictiveness; ≥4 symptoms, with someone other than a sibling. Conduct Disorder (312.8x/F91.x): a more severe pattern violating others' basic rights or major norms; ≥3 of 15 behaviours in 12 months across four groups: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations. ODD can precede CD; CD with callous-unemotional traits carries the highest risk. Nepal In Nepali classrooms the ADHD child is \"badmash\", the autistic child \"lato\", the child with ID \"kamjor\": moral labels where clinical ones belong; childhood depression hides behind stomachaches and irritability. School screening and teacher psychoeducation are high-leverage counselor tasks. Disability identity cards (four classes: red, blue, yellow, white) unlock allowances and school support: helping a family obtain one is a concrete counseling outcome. A worked example: A 9-year-old named Kiran fidgets constantly, blurts answers, and cannot finish assignments. His teacher calls him \"badmash,\" but a counselor identifies six inattention and six hyperactivity symptoms present before age 12, in both home and school, confirming the diagnosis. The intervention focuses on task breakdown and positive reinforcement rather than moral correction. Four disorders, one map. Top row is how the brain developed; bottom row is a learned pattern that can escalate, ODD sometimes preceding conduct disorder. In a Nepali classroom all four arrive with a moral label first. A skill to try · two minutes Co-regulation: lend your calm. A distressed child borrows an adult'"},{"u":"eating.html","te":"Eating disorders","tn":"","x":"Feeding & eating disorders Anorexia nervosa has one of the highest mortality rates of any mental disorder: around 5% per decade. Never manage it alone; refer medically early. Fainting, chest pain, a very slow heartbeat, or not keeping food or water down is a medical emergency: go to a hospital today. For low mood or thoughts of self-harm, call 1166 . The three-question sorting of the big eating disorders. Low weight takes priority once the anorexia criteria (fear of gaining, disturbed body experience) are met: a person of significantly low weight who binges and purges has anorexia (binge/purge subtype), not bulimia."},{"u":"eating.html#eating-c1","te":"Eating disorders","tn":"","x":"The criteria in brief; AN · BN · BED F50.0x · F50.2 · F50.81 Beyond curriculum Anorexia Nervosa Restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight for age, sex, development, and health. Intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain, even at low weight. Disturbance in how weight or shape is experienced, undue influence of weight/shape on self-evaluation, or persistent lack of recognition of the seriousness of the low weight. Subtypes: Restricting Binge-eating/purging Severity by BMI. Medical red flags: Bradycardia Lanugo QTc prolongation Bone loss Bulimia Nervosa Recurrent binge episodes: eating, in a discrete period, an amount definitely larger than most people would, with a sense of loss of control. Recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour: self-induced vomiting, laxatives/diuretics, fasting, excessive exercise. Both at least once a week for 3 months . Self-evaluation unduly influenced by shape and weight. Not exclusively during episodes of anorexia nervosa. Purging dangers: Hypokalaemia (arrhythmia) Metabolic alkalosis Dental erosion Russell's sign (knuckle calluses) Binge-Eating Disorder Recurrent binge episodes (as in bulimia). ≥3 of 5 : eating much more rapidly than normal · until uncomfortably full · large amounts when not hungry · alone out of embarrassment · disgust, depression, or guilt afterward. Marked distress about the bingeing. At least once a week for 3 months . No regular compensatory behaviour; not exclusively during bulimia or anorexia. Also in this chapter: ARFID (avoidant/restrictive intake without body-image disturbance) Pica (eating non-food substances ≥1 month, developmentally inappropriate, not culturally supported ) Rumination disorder (repeated regurgitation) International Long framed as a Western thin-ideal illness, but Hong Kong research (Sing Lee) described non-fat-phobic anorexia : restriction explained as stomach bloating or loss of appetite, no expressed fat fear, which DSM-5's Criterion B accommodates via \"persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain\". Eating disorders are rising fastest in Asia's urbanising economies. Nepal No national prevalence figure exists. Expect the non-fat-phobic presentation; distinguish disorder from poverty-driven undernutrition and from religious fasting (bartha): DSM-5 excludes culturally sanctioned practice. Urban private-school girls and gym-going young men (muscle-focused restriction, supplements) are the visible emerging groups. Weight loss + amenorrhoea in a young woman deserves an eating-disorder question before a TB work-up ends the conversation. A skill to try · two minutes A self-compassion pause. One hand over the heart. Three quiet lines: \"This is a hard moment. Hard moments are part of being human. May I be kind to myself in this one.\" Body-focused meditation can backfire here, so the kindness goes to the person, not the mirror. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it"},{"u":"sleep.html","te":"Sleep-wake disorders","tn":"","x":"Sleep-wake disorders Sleep is the most sensitive indicator of a client's mental state, and the thing you can most reliably improve in the first three sessions. DACUM lists \"assess sleep disturbances\" and \"assess nightmare\" as separate tasks. One night, mapped. Deep non-REM sleep piles up in the first third of the night: that is where sleep terrors and sleepwalking erupt, with no memory after. REM stretches lengthen toward morning: that is where nightmares (remembered vividly) and REM sleep behaviour disorder live. The exam question \"terror or nightmare?\" is answered by the clock and the memory."},{"u":"sleep.html#sleep-c1","te":"Sleep-wake disorders","tn":"","x":"Insomnia Disorder; the one you will meet most 307.42 · F51.01 Beyond curriculum DSM-5 Criteria Dissatisfaction with sleep quantity or quality, with one or more of: difficulty initiating sleep · difficulty maintaining sleep · early-morning awakening without return to sleep. Clinically significant distress or impairment (daytime fatigue, concentration, mood, work). At least 3 nights per week ; for at least 3 months ; despite adequate opportunity for sleep. Not better explained by another sleep-wake disorder; not attributable to a substance; coexisting mental and medical conditions do not adequately explain it. Disorder Code Core feature Hypersomnolence disorder F51.11 Excessive sleepiness despite ≥7 h sleep; unrefreshing naps Narcolepsy G47.41x Irresistible sleep attacks + cataplexy / hypocretin deficiency / SOREMPs Obstructive sleep apnoea G47.33 ≥5 apnoeas-hypopnoeas/hour + snoring or daytime sleepiness, or ≥15/hour regardless of symptoms Circadian rhythm disorders G47.2x Body clock misaligned with required schedule (shift work, delayed phase) NREM arousal disorders F51.3/.4 Sleepwalking · sleep terrors: first third of night, no recall Nightmare disorder F51.5 Extended dysphoric dreams, second half of night, vivid recall REM sleep behaviour disorder G47.52 Dream enactment: REM without muscle paralysis Restless legs syndrome G25.81 Urge to move legs, worse at rest and night, relieved by movement Nepal \"Nindra lagdaina\" is among the most common presenting complaints in Nepali primary care, and often the socially acceptable doorway to depression, anxiety, or trauma underneath. Ask about tea (strong, late, many cups), load-shedding-era phone habits, shift work in security and transport jobs, and crowded sleeping rooms. Sleep hygiene plus stimulus control is a counselor-deliverable intervention with fast, credibility-building results. A skill to try · two minutes The wind-down hour. Screens away one hour before bed; same wake time daily, even after a bad night; the 4-in 6-out breath once in bed. Awake more than 20 minutes? Get up, sit in dim light, return only when sleepy: the bed must mean sleep, not struggle. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. Key points Insomnia is trouble falling or staying asleep at least three nights a week for three months, with daytime cost. Sleep is the most sensitive sign of mental state and the easiest thing to improve early in counselling. Same wake time daily, screens away, bed only for sleep: the wind-down routine beats sleeping pills over time. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Insomnia disorder's thresholds: 3 nights a week for 3 months Every night for 2 weeks 1 night a week for a year ✔ Yes. Three and three, despite adequate opportunity to sleep. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Sleep terrors differ from nightmares because terrors:"},{"u":"substance.html","te":"Substance use","tn":"","x":"Substance-related & addictive disorders A CTEVT PSC curriculum topic (substance abuse and the stages of change). DSM-5 merged the old \"abuse\" and \"dependence\" into one graded disorder per substance. Shaking, sweating, confusion, seeing things, or a fit after stopping heavy drinking or sleeping pills is withdrawal and can be dangerous: go to a hospital today. For thoughts of self-harm, call 1166 ."},{"u":"substance.html#substance-c1","te":"Substance use","tn":"","x":"Substance Use Disorder; the 11-criteria frame e.g. alcohol · F10.x CTEVT PSC curriculum A problematic pattern of use causing clinically significant impairment or distress, shown by ≥2 of 11 criteria within 12 months . Severity: 2–3: mild 4–5: moderate ≥6: severe The same frame applies to alcohol, opioids, cannabis, stimulants, sedatives and tobacco. Gambling disorder sits beside them as the one behavioural addiction. Impaired control (1–4) More/longer than intended Wants to cut down, can't Much time obtaining, using, recovering Craving Social impairment (5–7) Fails role obligations Continues despite relationship problems Gives up important activities Risky use (8–9) Use in hazardous situations Continues despite physical/psychological harm Pharmacological (10–11) Tolerance: needs more for the same effect Withdrawal, or use to avoid withdrawal Nepal · International Globally, alcohol and tobacco dwarf all illicit drugs in disease burden. In Nepal, alcohol sits inside culture unevenly, customary in some communities, prohibited in others, so \"how much is a problem\" must be judged by the 11 criteria, not by drinking per se. Home-brew (raksi, jaand) makes quantity hard to estimate; ask in local units. Watch returned migrant workers, transport and security workers; and screen for depression and suicide risk alongside alcohol in every assessment. Pair the criteria with the Stages of Change (precontemplation → contemplation → preparation → action → maintenance → relapse) from your Month-4 block for counseling planning. The addiction cycle (after Koob & Volkow's three-stage model). Counselling enters at the craving stage: mapping each person's cues and building a plan for the moment the tea shop comes into view, which is why \"just promise to stop\" fails and cue-plans work. The stages of change (Prochaska and DiClemente). The counselor's job changes with the stage: information, then weighing, then planning, then support. Relapse re-enters the wheel; it is expected, not failure. A skill to try · two minutes Ride the craving. A craving crests and passes, often within 20 to 30 minutes; the cycle diagram above shows the moment it strikes. Delay (10 minutes first), drink water, move your feet, phone one safe person. You do not have to defeat the wave, only outlast it. This is for cravings; shaking, sweating, confusion or fits after stopping heavy alcohol or sleeping-pill use is withdrawal, and needs a doctor. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. Key points Substance use disorder is a pattern of use causing harm, judged by 11 criteria, not by how much someone drinks. Addiction is a cycle: use, withdrawal, craving, use again; tolerance rises and control falls each turn. Meet the client at their stage of change; withdrawal from heavy alcohol or sedatives can be dangerous and needs a doctor. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you"},{"u":"wellbeing.html","te":"Wellbeing & intervention","tn":"","x":"Psychosocial wellbeing & intervention \"Psychosocial\" is one word on purpose: the psychological (thoughts, emotions, behaviour) and the social (family, community, economy, culture) shape each other constantly. Intervention means acting on both sides at once, and mostly through communities, not clinics."},{"u":"wellbeing.html#wellbeing-c1","te":"Wellbeing & intervention","tn":"","x":"The pyramid of need and intervention CTEVT PSC curriculum The intervention pyramid (after Galappatti and the IASC guidelines). Most people are served by the bottom two layers alone; the psychosocial counselor's own tier is the third; only a few clients ever need the psychiatric top, and your referral is their bridge. A worked example: A 40-year-old named Goma lost her home in a flood. The counselor uses the pyramid to explain: Goma needs basic services first (shelter, food), then community supports (a women's group), before any specialized counseling. The counselor's own role sits at the community tier, mobilizing what Goma already has rather than importing outside expertise. Core ideas the curriculum names: psychosocial wellbeing has dimensions (biological, emotional, social, cultural, spiritual, material); intervention can be individual or community-based (CBI) : CBI mobilises the community's own resiliency rather than importing outside experts. A rights-based approach treats women's, children's and elders' basic rights as the floor of wellbeing. A multidisciplinary holistic approach means the counselor, health worker, teacher, lawyer, and social worker act as one linked system, which is why referral, coordination and linkage get their own page."},{"u":"wellbeing.html#wellbeing-c2","te":"Wellbeing & intervention","tn":"","x":"The layers around a person, and what wellbeing is made of CTEVT PSC curriculum Bronfenbrenner's social-ecological model reads a life as rings around the person. A counselor who only works the innermost ring misses where most Nepali distress is made and where much of it can be eased. Ring What sits there A Nepali example Individual Temperament, health, coping style, personal history A quiet girl who somatises worry as stomach pain Microsystem Settings the person is directly in: family, school, workplace, peers A joint household; a teacher who calls the child \"lato\" Mesosystem Links between those settings Whether the school ever talks to the parents Exosystem Settings that shape life without the person being in them The father's job in Qatar; the ward office; local media Macrosystem Culture, law, economy, shared beliefs Caste, dowry expectations, karma explanations, the migration economy The rings around a person (Bronfenbrenner). Read a client outward: who is in the house, whether the school and the family talk, what the migration economy did to the household, what caste and karma explanations do to help-seeking. What wellbeing is made of (the elements the curriculum lists): psychological includes self-acceptance, autonomy, environmental mastery, purpose, personal growth, and emotional self-regulation. Social includes warm relationships, belonging, contributing something, trust and safety in one's community, and cultural and spiritual connectedness. Structural includes physical safety, food, housing, livelihood, and access to health, education and legal protection. The bottom row is the floor the other two stand on."},{"u":"wellbeing.html#wellbeing-c3","te":"Wellbeing & intervention","tn":"","x":"Community-based support: principles and a roadmap CTEVT PSC curriculum Community-based MHPSS treats the community as the agent of its own recovery, not the audience for outside experts. Its principles: do no harm , meaningful participation , build local capacity , cultural fit , and inclusion of the marginalised. Modalities you will use include Psychological First Aid, psychoeducation, structured groups, task-shared protocols such as WHO's Problem Management Plus (PM+), and livelihood support joined to peer groups. Step What you do Looks like 1 · Assess with the community Focus groups, key informants, a map of assets and existing coping Who do people already go to? Mothers' groups, the health post, the jhankri 2 · Co-design Adapt materials with a local committee: language, metaphors, timing \"Man\" not \"mental health\" on the poster; sessions after the harvest 3 · Build capacity, share tasks Train gatekeepers and volunteers in basics, PFA, groups, safe referral Female community health volunteers who can spot risk and refer 4 · Run core activities Peer groups, child-friendly spaces, cultural and recreational events A women's circle; a safe space in the school after the flood 5 · Referral pathways Link to the health system for those who need layer 3 or 4 care A named person and number at the nearest hospital 6 · Monitor together Track outcomes with validated tools and community feedback Nepali PHQ-9 or GAD-7 at start and end; a review meeting the group owns Key points Psychosocial means mind and social world shaping each other; intervention works on both at once. The IASC pyramid: most people need only basic services and community support; the counselor works in the third layer; few need specialists. Community-based support builds on what the community already has: do no harm, involve everyone, train local people. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. The base of the intervention pyramid is: Specialised psychiatric care Basic services and security for everyone Individual counselling ✔ Yes. Food, shelter, safety and information prevent most distress. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. The psychosocial counselor works mostly at: The top tier The focused, non-specialised tier Outside the pyramid ✔ Yes. Counselling and PFA follow-up sit on tier three. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Community-based intervention (CBI) primarily: Imports outside experts Mobilises the community's own resiliency Builds new hospitals ✔ Yes. Groups, rituals, leaders: the community already holds resources. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Learn more Pick one, not all. Guideline IASC Guidelines on Mental Health and Psychosocial Support in Emergency Settings (2007) : the source of the pyramid; skim the action sheets. Paper Galappatti (2003), \"What is a psychosocial intervention?\", In"},{"u":"approaches.html","te":"Approaches & forms","tn":"","x":"Counselling: approaches, forms & what it is Counselling is a structured, confidential helping relationship in which a trained person helps a client understand their situation and find their own way through it. It is NOT advice-giving, lecturing, interrogation, or ordinary chat, and the client, not the counselor, remains the expert on their own life."},{"u":"approaches.html#approaches-c1","te":"Approaches & forms","tn":"","x":"Six approaches: one table CTEVT PSC curriculum Approach Problem is… Counselor does… Best for · watch out Psychodynamic Unconscious conflict rooted in early relationships Listens for patterns, explores childhood, works with the relationship itself Long-standing relational patterns · slow, needs training Behavioural Learned behaviour kept alive by reinforcement Exposure, activity scheduling, reinforcement of new behaviour Phobia, avoidance, habits · ignores meaning if used alone Humanistic (Rogers) Growth blocked by lack of acceptance Empathy, genuineness, unconditional positive regard; client leads The foundation of ALL counselling · not enough alone in severe illness Cognitive-behavioural (CBT) Unhelpful thoughts drive feelings and behaviour Identify → test → reframe distortions; homework; behavioural experiments Depression, anxiety · needs literacy-adapted tools in Nepal Exposure technique Avoidance keeps fear alive (the loop of {{section:foundation}}) Graded ladder; stay long enough to learn that the feared outcome is less likely or more bearable than predicted (anxiety may or may not fall); repeat Phobia, OCD; PTSD only as trauma-focused therapy by a trained provider · never force; consent and pacing are everything Socio-cultural Distress produced by context: poverty, discrimination, family, culture Works with family/community, healers, resources, advocacy Almost every Nepali case · don't let it excuse skipping the individual The table is the map a first reader needs. The four sections below open one approach at a time, at the depth the curriculum expects for the exam. Go deeper: the psychodynamic approach CTEVT PSC curriculum Freud's two models in one picture. The classroom example: hungry in an exam hall, the id says eat now, the ego says wait thirty minutes and eat safely, the superego says copying a friend's answer to finish early would be wrong. The ego is the part doing the negotiating. The drawing simplifies: in Freud's own account the ego and superego both reach down into the unconscious. Three assumptions carry the whole approach: Psychic determinism : thoughts, feelings and slips have causes, often out of awareness Unconscious motivation : drives we do not see steer us Childhood primacy : early relationships shape adult patterns The counselor listens for repeating patterns, especially in how the client treats the counselor. Psychosexual stages , as the curriculum expects them. Freud held that pleasure-seeking energy settles on one body zone per stage, and that an unresolved stage leaves a \"fixation\" in the adult. Learn the table for the exam; then hold it lightly: neither the stages nor the popular \"id-dominant / superego-dominant personality\" typology is diagnostic, and neither has held up as evidence. What survives is the wider idea that early relationships and defences matter. Stage · age Zone · task \"Fixation\" traits Freud proposed Oral · birth to 18 months Mouth · weaning Dependence, overeating, smoking; or sarcasm and verbal ho"},{"u":"approaches.html#approaches-c2","te":"Approaches & forms","tn":"","x":"Seven forms of counselling · three modes CTEVT PSC curriculum Form When Key precaution Individual The default: one client, private space Confidentiality; records with codes, not names Family Problem lives between members (a child's refusal, an elder's care) Define who the client is; stay neutral between members Group Shared problems: grief, addiction recovery, GBV survivors Screen members; group confidentiality agreement Crisis Immediately after acute events; safety first, feelings second Short, practical, directive is OK here Supportive Ongoing emotional support where cure is not the goal (chronic illness, caregivers) Don't promise change; prevent dependency Couple / marriage Relationship distress Balance; screen for violence FIRST: couple work is unsafe amid GBV Task-oriented One concrete goal (exam panic, a decision) Keep scope narrow; finish and close Three modes (know the difference for the exam): Advice-oriented : telling what to do. Sometimes needed in crisis, but not counselling proper. Informative : giving accurate information, such as HIV facts or service directions. Psychological or psychosocial counselling : the real craft, helping the client find their own solution. Most clients arrive expecting advice. Part of session one is teaching what counselling is. Test yourself 1) A wife reports beatings and asks for couple counselling, which form do you refuse for now, and what do you do instead? 2) Match approach to case: a bus-phobic teacher; a farmer whose \"man is heavy\" with automatic thoughts of worthlessness; a possession episode the family reads spiritually. 3) Why is \"counselling = giving good advice\" a misconception? Key points Counselling is a structured, confidential relationship that helps people find their own way; it is not advice or lecturing. Six approaches (psychodynamic, behavioural, humanistic, cognitive-behavioural, exposure, socio-cultural) each explain suffering differently and offer different tools; the humanistic conditions underlie all of them. Forms of counselling (individual, group, family, crisis, peer...) are chosen by the problem and the setting, not by habit. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Rogers' three core conditions are: Advice, homework, testing Empathy, genuineness, unconditional positive regard Silence, notes, referral ✔ Yes. They are the foundation of every counselling approach. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. A wife reports beatings and requests couple counselling. You: Start couple sessions Decline couple work for now; safety and individual support first Ask the husband to explain ✔ Yes. Couple counselling amid active violence is unsafe for the survivor. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Counselling proper means: Giving good advice Helping the client find their own solution Filling forms ✔ Yes. The client stays the expert on"},{"u":"skills.html","te":"Communication skills","tn":"","x":"Communication skills: SOLER and the seventeen 99 of your 780 hours go here, more than any disorder gets, because skills, not knowledge, are what the client experiences. Non-verbal skills open the door; verbal skills do the work."},{"u":"skills.html#skills-c1","te":"Communication skills","tn":"","x":"The non-verbal foundation CTEVT PSC curriculum Before a single skill, the body is already speaking. Non-verbal presence does three jobs: it makes the room feel safe , it shows congruence , and it co-regulates . It makes the room safe because a calm, receptive stance tells a nervous client that nothing here will be judged. It shows congruence: when your words and your posture agree, the client trusts both; when they disagree, the client believes the posture. It co-regulates: a grounded posture, steady breathing and a gentle voice slowly settle an activated client, the way a calm adult settles a frightened child. SOLER S it squarely facing the client O pen posture (no crossed arms) L ean slightly toward them E ye contact, natural and culturally tuned R elax Around it sit three more channels. Affirming : nodding and humming \"hmm\" Silence : the hardest skill, waiting while the client finds words Observation : posture, tears held back, hands, and what is not said Nepali tuning matters here. Sustained direct eye contact from a young counselor to an elder, or across gender, can read as disrespect; soften it. A client may talk most freely while looking away. Beyond SOLER, four channels carry most of what a client reads in you. None of them needs a number attached; they need attention. A worked example: A counselor named Sunita meets a client who speaks softly and avoids eye contact. Sunita sits at an angle, keeps her hands visible, and lets silence stand for ten seconds. The client gradually says, \"I have never told anyone this.\" The non-verbal choices made space; the words followed. Channel Do Watch out Body and face Small, timed nods that say \"go on\"; a face that stays warm and matches the client's tone; let your posture follow theirs a little rather than sit like a statue The flicker of shock, disgust or judgement on your face when trauma or a stigmatised act is disclosed; the client sees it before you feel it Voice Warm, unhurried; slow down and soften in the intense moments; let a pause stand, the client is working in it Filling every silence; speeding up when you are anxious; a bright cheerful tone over heavy material Space About an arm's length or more, tuned to the person and the culture; sit at an angle or side by side, nothing between you: no desk, no file on your lap A desk between you turns the session into an interview; crowding a trauma survivor, or a woman meeting a male counselor, closes the door Touch Only if culturally safe and asked for. Warmth travels through voice, face and posture; those are enough A hand on the shoulder can revive a trauma, blur the boundary, or offend across gender or caste; when in doubt, offer water instead Practice drill: watch yourself Record a ten-minute role-play, or ask a classmate to watch, and tick honestly: Face and eyes Eye contact soft and steady, not a stare, not darting away Face warm and matching the client's tone No flicker of shock or judgement at the hard moments Nods small and timed Posture and "},{"u":"skills.html#skills-c2","te":"Communication skills","tn":"","x":"The verbal seventeen: with one example each Nine of these seventeen are worked in full, with steps, phrases and the mistakes to avoid: the five reflecting skills on {{page:skills-listening}}, the four change skills on {{page:skills-moving}}. The other eight live only in this table. Skill What it is Sounds like Questioning Open questions invite; closed questions confirm \"What was that time like for you?\" vs \"Did you sleep?\" Paraphrasing Their content, your fresh words \"So since the loan came due, the nights are the worst.\" Reflection of feeling Naming the emotion under the words \"You sound frightened, and a little angry too.\" Reflection of meaning Naming what it means to them \"Failing the exam feels like failing your whole family.\" Repetition of key words Echo the loaded word to open it Client: \"…then everything finished.\" You: \"Finished?\" Summarising Gathering threads at turns and closings \"Let me pull together the three things you've named…\" Challenge Gently naming a contradiction \"You say it doesn't matter, yet you cry when you say it.\" Feedback Sharing what you observe, without verdicts \"Today you looked at the floor when your father came up.\" Information giving Accurate, needed, digestible facts \"Panic symptoms are frightening but not dangerous: here is why.\" Self-disclosure A brief, purposeful sliver of your own experience: for the client's sake, rarely \"I too left my village for work; the first months were hard.\" Psychoeducation Teaching the mechanism behind the symptom The fight-or-flight body map of {{section:foundation}}, drawn on paper Brainstorming Generating options before judging any \"List every possibility first, even the silly ones.\" Suggest and fade Offer an option, hand ownership back \"Some people try X: what would fit you?\" Empathy Feeling-with communicated accurately Not \"I understand\" but proving it in your reflection Consoling & appropriate touch Water, tissue, presence; touch only if culturally safe Silence + offered water often consoles more than words Interpreting non-verbals Reading and gently naming body signals \"Your fists closed when we mentioned the school.\" Active listening The umbrella: all of the above, together, honestly The client's test: \"Did this person actually hear me?\" Practice drill Client says: \"Since my husband went to Qatar I don't sleep, my chest burns, and my mother-in-law says I am pretending.\" Write one open question, one paraphrase, one reflection of feeling, one reflection of meaning, and one gentle challenge: then check each against the table. The micro-skills ladder. The two highlighted steps, attending and reflecting, carry most of a session; questioning comes third on purpose. Beginners reverse the order and interrogate. Key points Skills, not knowledge, are what the client experiences; 99 of the 780 course hours are spent here. Non-verbal attending (posture, eye contact, silence) opens the door; verbal skills (reflecting, paraphrasing, open questions, summarising) do the work. Reflect feeling "},{"u":"skills-listening.html","te":"Listening back: reflecting skills","tn":"","x":"Listening back: the reflecting skills The table on {{page:skills}} names seventeen verbal skills. Five of them make a client feel heard, and they are worked in full here: what to do, what to say, and the mistakes new counselors make. One fictional student, a composite invented for teaching and not a client, runs through the first four cards so the skills can be seen building on each other: from a village, alone in a rented room in Kathmandu, grades low, parents in debt for the fees. The four skills that help a client move are on {{page:skills-moving}}; the other eight of the seventeen stay in the table."},{"u":"skills-listening.html#skills-listening-c1","te":"Listening back: reflecting skills","tn":"","x":"1 · Paraphrasing: their content, your words CTEVT PSC curriculum Giving back the main content of what the client said, shorter and in your own plain words, so they hear: I listened, and I understood it the way you meant it. Listen for the key message. Translate it into your own clear, respectful words. Check , gently, whether you got it right. Say: \"So what you are saying is…\" · \"If I have understood you right, you…\" · \"From what I hear, your main worry is that…\" Student: \"I have to give a presentation. I feel my friends laugh at my English. I think about it so much I cannot sleep.\" Counselor: \"If I have understood you right, worrying about how they will take your English is making the presentation hard to prepare, and costing you sleep too, is that it?\" What goes wrong: Parroting : their exact words back (\"So… presentation, friends laugh, no sleep?\"). Adding a guess : \"Is it because your English is weak?\" They never said that. Advice : \"Then practise in front of a mirror.\" Feelings only : \"You are very afraid of your friends\" drops the content; that is the next skill."},{"u":"skills-listening.html#skills-listening-c2","te":"Listening back: reflecting skills","tn":"","x":"2 · Reflection of feeling: the heart under the words CTEVT PSC curriculum Paraphrasing touches the head; this touches the heart. Name the feeling the client expressed or showed (fear, sadness, anger, helplessness, guilt), so they feel someone has felt it with them. Read more than the words: tone, face, tears held back. Name the feeling tentatively, not as a verdict. Check : \"…is that how it is?\" Say: \"Listening to you, it seems you are feeling quite…\" · \"This seems to have left you very…\" · \"From your voice there is a lot of… inside, is that so?\" Student (looking down, close to tears): \"In the village I was always with friends. Here I come back to this room alone. Sometimes I want to cry.\" Counselor: \"Living alone in this big city after that warmth, loneliness and missing home seem to be weighing on you, and under it a sadness, is that right?\" What goes wrong: Minimising : \"Crying over that? Everyone feels it at first in Kathmandu.\" Labeling : \"You are clearly in clinical depression.\" You reflect a feeling, not a diagnosis. Sympathy, not empathy : \"Oh, poor you.\" Pity stands above; empathy sits beside. Chasing facts : \"Which college? Which part of town?\" Feelings first."},{"u":"skills-listening.html#skills-listening-c3","te":"Listening back: reflecting skills","tn":"","x":"3 · Reflection of meaning: the \"why\" behind the feeling CTEVT PSC curriculum Head, heart, and now the person's values: what the event means to them, for their life and self-respect. When an event strikes a value or a hope, that is where the pain comes from. Listen for expectations, beliefs, values hidden in the story. Name the meaning , with words like \"means\", \"matters\", \"in your eyes\". Check whether it lands. Say: \"For you, the deeper meaning of this is…\" · \"It sounds as if this is what matters most in your life, and now…\" · \"This seems to have shaken your trust in yourself, is that so?\" Student: \"My parents took a loan to send me to this expensive college, and my first GPA came out very low. When I picture their faces I cannot sleep.\" Counselor: \"Their sacrifice and their trust in you weigh more than anything. So a low result means, in your eyes, letting them down, and that is what makes it unbearable, is that right?\" What goes wrong: Imposing your values : \"Grades are not everything!\" For them, study may be their whole world. Staying on the surface : \"You feel sad and afraid.\" True, but the trust and sacrifice are missed. Judging the value : \"It is wrong to stress yourself so much over your parents.\""},{"u":"skills-listening.html#skills-listening-c4","te":"Listening back: reflecting skills","tn":"","x":"4 · Summarising: gathering the threads CTEVT PSC curriculum Pulling the facts, feelings and meanings of a stretch of talk into one short, ordered summary. Three moments call for it: the start of a session, a turning point before a new topic or solutions, and the close. Pick the milestones only, two or three. Order them : first, second, third, and any decision taken. Hand it back : \"Have I got it, or is something missing?\" Say: \"To put what we have covered so far in short…\" · \"You have raised several important things; if I gather them…\" · \"…did I sum that up rightly, or is there something to add?\" Counselor (closing): \"Three things came up today. First, the sadness of arriving from the village into this loneliness. Second, the worry that the low GPA has broken your parents' trust. Third, what the stress is doing to your sleep. And we decided you will try one college club this week. Have I got it right?\" Student: \"Yes. Hearing it like that, it feels lighter.\" What goes wrong: Overloading : \"You came on Sunday, got off at Sundhara, looked for a room…\" A list, not a summary. Distorting : \"…and you have depression, so you are thinking of going home.\" They never said that. Skipping confirmation : rattling it off and standing up turns a shared summary into a speech."},{"u":"skills-listening.html#skills-listening-c5","te":"Listening back: reflecting skills","tn":"","x":"5 · Repetition of key words: one word, held out CTEVT PSC curriculum Echoing exactly one word the client leaned on (a feeling, a decision, a confusion), with a slight lift of question. It invites more without interrupting, and slows the talk so they can look at that word again. Catch the last word, or the heaviest one. Return it softly, one word only. Wait ; the silence does the rest. Say: \"…finished?\" · \"…alone?\" · \"…confused?\" Client: \"My scholarship did not come through. I cannot face asking my parents. I feel completely ruined.\" Counselor: \"…ruined?\" Client: \"Yes, because if I cannot pay the fees my studies stop here, and there is nothing left but going back to the village.\" What goes wrong: Overuse : echoing every sentence (\"College?\" \"Friend?\" \"Went home?\") sounds like a robot. Interrogative tone : \"Ruined?? How, ruined? Tell me!\" is a police question. The wrong word : \"bus\" or \"tea\" instead of \"fear\", \"pressure\", \"decision\". Practice drill With a classmate, run the four-card thread (arrival, loneliness, the GPA, the loan) as one twenty-minute session: paraphrase, reflect feeling, reflect meaning, summarise. Record it and listen back for the named mistakes. Key points Paraphrase returns the content, reflection of feeling the emotion, reflection of meaning the value underneath; each ends with a check question, never a verdict. A single word held out, softly, opens more than a question; a summary gathers two or three milestones at the start, a turning point, or the close, and always asks \"have I got it?\" Every reflecting skill has a named way of failing: parroting, adding a guess, giving advice, minimising, labeling, staying on the surface, overloading. Knowing the mistake by name is half of avoiding it. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. The client says: \"I cannot sleep, my friends laugh at my English.\" The counselor replies: \"So… you cannot sleep and your friends laugh at your English?\" This is: A good paraphrase Parroting Reflection of meaning ✔ Yes. The words came back unchanged; a paraphrase puts the content into your own fresh words and checks. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. The client says: \"I feel completely ruined.\" The counselor says softly: \"…ruined?\" This is: Repetition of a key word Challenge Summarising ✔ Yes. One heavy word, held out with a slight lift, invites the client to say more. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Summarising belongs at three moments. Which is NOT one of them? The start of a session A turning point before a new topic While the client is mid-sentence ✔ Yes. A summary gathers threads at the start, a turn, or the close; it never interrupts. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Learn more Pick one, not all. Book Allen Ivey and colleagues, Intentional Interviewing and Counseling : the micro-skills tradition these "},{"u":"skills-moving.html","te":"Moving forward: change skills","tn":"","x":"Moving forward: the change skills Once a client feels heard ({{page:skills-listening}}), four skills help them move: generating options, seeing a contradiction, getting the facts, and understanding their own body and mind. Each card gives what to do, what to say, and the mistakes new counselors make. The situations are short, fictional, and different on each card."},{"u":"skills-moving.html#skills-moving-c1","te":"Moving forward: change skills","tn":"","x":"1 · Brainstorming: many options before any judgement CTEVT PSC curriculum Counselor and client together make the longest possible list of ways through a problem, then choose. Four rules, explained to the client first: no judgement while listing; free thinking, odd and small ideas count; quantity over quality, five or six; combine and improve. State the problem in one clear sentence. List options , pen and paper out, no criticism; the client leads. Weigh pros and cons, then choose the most practical first step. Say: \"Come, let us list together everything that might help.\" · \"Right now we are not sorting right from wrong; whatever comes to mind goes on the paper.\" · \"What could be the first small step? Say it, whatever it is.\" Client: \"I feel very alone here.\" Counselor: \"Let us brainstorm; no judging yet, only listing. What comes first?\" Client: \"A college club?\" Counselor: \"On the list. I will add one: a twenty-minute evening walk. What else?\" Client: \"Calling old friends twice a week.\" Counselor: \"Three. Two more, then we pick the first to try.\" What goes wrong: Immediate criticism : \"A walk? How will that cure loneliness?\" The client stops thinking. The expert trap : \"Here are my five ideas: first…\" Direction breeds dependence. No filtering : a long list and no chosen first step."},{"u":"skills-moving.html#skills-moving-c2","te":"Moving forward: change skills","tn":"","x":"2 · Challenge: holding up a gentle mirror CTEVT PSC curriculum The most delicate skill and one of the most powerful. Challenge (the curriculum also says confrontation) is never proving the client wrong; it is placing a mismatch in front of them, respectfully, so they can see it. Three mismatches: words against actions (\"I want to top the class\", then a day on the phone); words against feelings (\"I am fine\", eyes full of tears); one statement against another. It needs strong rapport; too early, it frightens the client away. Acknowledge the good in what they said. Name both sides : \"On one hand…, on the other hand…\", softly. Hand it back : \"How do you see these two together?\" Say: \"On one hand you say…, but on the other hand in what you do…\" · \"I feel a little puzzled: earlier you said…, and now…\" · \"You smile and say you are fine, yet your eyes look sad. How do these two fit?\" Client (who earlier admitted five or six hours a day on social media): \"I have to get a good GPA this semester. Study is everything to me.\" Counselor: \"That commitment is real. I notice one small mismatch: on one hand you want a good result more than anything, on the other hand five or six hours a day go to social media. How do those two sit together?\" Client (after a pause): \"You are right. I keep saying I will study, and the time keeps going.\" What goes wrong: Accusing : \"You are lying to yourself! Facebook all day and you expect marks?\" An attack closes the door. Hard tone : any hint of \"I will fix you\" turns the mirror into a weapon. Too early : safety first, mirror later."},{"u":"skills-moving.html#skills-moving-c3","te":"Moving forward: change skills","tn":"","x":"3 · Information giving: facts that open doors CTEVT PSC curriculum Giving the client the facts, procedures and resources they need to decide for themselves, and pointing to the right place when a referral is needed. Information offers choices; advice takes them away. Check that it is accurate and from a real source. Give only what is needed now, in plain words. Ask what they made of it, and whether they want the next step. Say: \"There are a few options and resources for this I can tell you about…\" · \"As far as I know, the process is…\" · \"Some colleges have support for students in this situation. Would you like to know how it works?\" Client: \"Rent and fees have come due at once. I cannot ask my parents. I am close to leaving college.\" Counselor: \"Before deciding, one piece of information: the college has a fund for students from outside the valley that covers part of the fees, and part-time posts in the library. Would you like to know how to apply?\" What goes wrong: Advice in disguise : \"Do not even think of leaving. Go to the office now, fill the form.\" Misinformation : \"I think the college teaches everyone for free.\" If you do not know, say so and find out. Overload : ten organisations and phone numbers to someone already overwhelmed."},{"u":"skills-moving.html#skills-moving-c4","te":"Moving forward: change skills","tn":"","x":"4 · Psychoeducation: \"what is happening to me, and why\" CTEVT PSC curriculum Close to information giving, but about the mind and body themselves. When a client is frightened by their own symptoms, you explain in simple, honest words what is happening and why. Three aims: normalise (a healthy body reacting to stress, not madness), reduce stigma, and hand over a first self-management skill such as slow breathing. The fight-or-flight map on {{section:foundation}}, drawn on paper, is the classic tool. Ask permission : \"May I explain a little of what the body does under stress?\" Explain with a daily-life picture, not textbook words. Check : \"How does that sound?\" and leave room for questions. Say: \"Under heavy stress the body and brain work in a particular way. May I tell you a little about it?\" · \"Once the doctor has checked the heart, a racing heart in anxiety is not a disease of the body; it is…\" · \"The brain has a kind of alarm bell; when it senses danger…\" Client: \"Before a presentation my heart pounds so hard I feel I am having a heart attack. Am I going mad?\" Counselor: \"First, new or strong chest symptoms always deserve a doctor's check, and yours came back clear. So this is not a disease of the body, and you are not going mad. When the brain reads a situation as a big threat, the body switches on fight-or-flight: adrenaline, a faster heart, shaking hands, sweat. A healthy body getting ready for danger. Once we learn a way to calm it, it settles. How does that sound?\" What goes wrong: Lecturing in jargon : \"Your sympathetic nervous system is activated.\" They feel smaller, not wiser. Labeling : \"You have anxiety disorder\" is a diagnosis as verdict, and not yours to give. One-way talk : ten minutes of you, never asking \"how much of this made sense?\" Practice drill With a classmate: one plays a student who says \"study is everything\" but spends evenings on the phone. Run brainstorming first (five options, no judging), then one gentle challenge in the \"on one hand, on the other\" frame, then one piece of information. Swap roles. Which of the named mistakes did each of you make? Key points Brainstorming has four rules the client hears first: no judging while listing, free thinking, quantity over quality, combine and improve; the client leads, and the session ends with one chosen first step. Challenge only after rapport, always in the \"on one hand, on the other hand\" frame, and handed back as a question; a hard tone turns the mirror into a weapon. Information gives options, advice takes them away; psychoeducation asks permission, explains in daily-life pictures, normalises, and hands over one first skill. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. During brainstorming the client suggests an evening walk. The counselor says: \"A walk? How will that cure loneliness?\" Which rule broke? No judging while listing Quantity over quality Combine and "},{"u":"process.html","te":"The counselling process","tn":"","x":"The counselling process: rapport to termination The curriculum breaks counselling into 24 tasks; they group into six stages. The craft is knowing which stage you are in, and not skipping ahead."},{"u":"process.html#process-c1","te":"The counselling process","tn":"","x":"Six stages, twenty-four tasks CTEVT PSC curriculum Stage Tasks (curriculum numbering) The one thing that matters most 1 · Build rapport Set environment · identify client · introduce yourself · explain confidentiality and its limits · ask expectations · explain what counselling is (1–6) Safety. No technique works before trust 2 · Assess Psychosocial problems (4 dimensions + frequency + intensity) · daily functioning · family environment · work/school · childhood & attachments · decision-making · suicidal thoughts: every client · coping · social support (me-map) · psychotic features (hallucination, delusion, orientation) (7–16) Function beats symptom-lists: what can the person no longer do? 3 · Formulate Identify core problems · make the 4-factor formulation (17–18) See the diagram below: this is the exam's favourite question 4 · Plan Set SMART goals with the client, prioritised (19) The client's goals, not yours 5 · Intervene Implement strategies · evaluate each session (20–21) Small, reviewed steps; adjust what isn't working 6 · Close Follow up · terminate by mutual agreement · manage the counselor-client relationship throughout (22–24) Endings are planned, never abrupt: abandonment harms The first two stages in words, the four opening scripts and the assessment table, are on {{page:first-sessions}}. Stages three to six are worked below the diagram. A worked example: A 22-year-old named Rohan presents with insomnia and exam anxiety. In stage two, his counselor assesses: he can no longer concentrate, he avoids friends (a maintaining loop), and his mother's encouragement is a protective factor. The counselor uses this to build the 4-P formulation rather than jumping straight to techniques. The 4-P formulation (curriculum task 18). Interventions map onto arrows: most predisposing factors are history and cannot be changed, though their present effects can; you can defuse triggers, you MUST break maintaining loops, and you always strengthen the protective corner. Test yourself Build a 4-P formulation: a 19-year-old with panic attacks that began after her SEE results, whose mother had \"nerves\", whose family now lets her avoid every bus, and who is close to her grandmother and good at tailoring. Then write one SMART goal with her."},{"u":"process.html#process-c2","te":"The counselling process","tn":"","x":"Before anyone speaks: the room (task 1) CTEVT PSC curriculum The curriculum's first task is not a skill but a space. Most of it costs nothing. Private: quiet, not overheard, door closed unless the client prefers it open, a \"session in progress\" sign if interruptions are common. Comfortable and safe: chairs at the same level, no desk between you, light and air that do not tire; nothing sharp or harmful within reach; child-sized furniture if you see children. Non-threatening: sit at an angle or side by side, about an arm's length or more, tuned to the person and the culture. Tidy: tissues, water and paper to hand; clutter raises tension. Accessible and respectful: reachable for a wheelchair; gender-appropriate seating; nothing on the walls that could offend. Warm: a greeting and a calm voice set the mood before any technique does. Quiet devices: phone silent; if you record or use video, it works and the client knows."},{"u":"process.html#process-c3","te":"The counselling process","tn":"","x":"From formulation to goodbye: stages 3 to 6 in words CTEVT PSC curriculum Core problem (task 17). Weigh each problem the client named by impact, urgency, and the client's own priority. Then ask, do not announce: \"From what we have discussed, the work stress, the arguments at home and the poor sleep all seem linked to the anxiety.\" Never let a hidden but serious issue sink below the client's stated priority. Formulation and strategy (task 18). The curriculum's four factors are vulnerable, triggering, maintaining and protective. Sort what you learned into four boxes, write a working hypothesis, and say it back for correction. \"Your anxiety started after you lost the job (the trigger), but it continues because you avoid people and stay in (what keeps it going). At the same time you have a sister who encourages you (a strength). So we could take small steps back toward people, lean on your sister, and work on the thoughts that stop you applying for work.\" Strategies match factors: defuse the trigger, break the maintaining loop, build on the protective corner. Keep it simple, share it, and update it when new facts arrive. Goals (task 19). Short-term steps inside a long-term aim, owned by the client, SMART. Vague: \"I want to feel better.\" Workable: \"For the next two weeks I will try a relaxation exercise every night before bed and keep a simple sleep diary.\" Then check: \"Does that sound manageable?\" A goal too big discourages; adjust it as life changes. Implement and evaluate (tasks 20 and 21). Teach the technique in the room, agree when and how often it is practised, and review it next session. Drop what is not working; one or two strategies at a time, never a list. Evaluation is a conversation, not a form: \"From your side, how have things changed since we started? Which areas feel better, and which still need work?\" Compare with the first assessment, take the client's view as central, and count small steps as progress. Follow-up and termination (tasks 22 and 23). Ending is planned from the start. Say in advance that sessions will close, review what was gained, make room for feelings about ending, and agree a plan for future rough patches. \"You are managing your challenges much better now, so we can begin to close our sessions. Before we finish, let's go over the skills you have built.\" Follow-up is agreed together, for example at one, three and six months, by visit or phone, and stays voluntary: it checks that gains hold and catches a relapse early, without making the client dependent. When the relationship itself wobbles (task 24). Six common strains appear: lost trust, resistance, dependency, a clash of values, a boundary crossed, a misunderstanding. Name the tension rather than ride over it: \"It seems there has been some discomfort in our sessions lately. I value your honesty, so could we talk about how you are feeling about our work together?\" Then revisit roles and confidentiality, stay consistent, keep the boundary, take it to supervision, and r"},{"u":"first-sessions.html","te":"The first sessions","tn":"","x":"The first sessions: what to say, what to ask {{page:process}} gives the map of the counselling process. This chapter is the first two stages in words: the four things you say before a client has told you anything, and the ten things you ask once they begin. The scripts are starting points; make them sound like you."},{"u":"first-sessions.html#first-sessions-c1","te":"The first sessions","tn":"","x":"Before the first word: who is in the room CTEVT PSC curriculum Curriculum task 2 is the basic map before the journey: who this person is (name, age, where they live, how to reach them, work or study, who referred them: self, family, school, health post) and why they have come , in their own words. Ask \"What brings you here today?\" and then stay quiet. Note when it started, whether it is getting worse, and what it is costing them day to day. The precaution is the same for every task on this page: do not assume, do not rush, and remember that people describe distress in their own idiom ( \"man dukhchha\" , a burning chest, a heavy head), not in yours. Records at this point: an intake form and a few lines of what the client said, filed under a code number, never a name."},{"u":"first-sessions.html#first-sessions-c2","te":"The first sessions","tn":"","x":"The first ten minutes, in words CTEVT PSC curriculum Tasks 3 to 6 happen in one flow, before the client has shared anything painful. Each has a script below and one precaution. Read them aloud until they stop sounding like a form. 1 · Introducing yourself (task 3) \"Namaste, please sit. I am [your name], a psychosocial counselor here. My work is to listen, to understand your situation with you, and to look together for what might help. I am not a doctor and I will not give medicines. Before we begin, how was the journey here? And how are you feeling right now?\" Precaution: short, plain, no titles you have not earned. A moment of small talk lowers the pulse; a paragraph about your qualifications raises it. 2 · Confidentiality and its limits (task 4) \"What you tell me here stays between us. I will not tell your family, your school, or anyone else without your permission. There are a few situations where I would have to involve someone, and I want you to know them now: if I believe you are in serious danger of harming yourself, if someone else is in serious danger, if a child or a person who cannot protect themselves is being abused, or if a court orders it. Even then, wherever it is safe and lawful, I would tell you first, and share only what is needed to keep people safe. Is that all right with you?\" Precaution: say this before the client discloses anything, and never promise a secrecy you cannot legally keep. In Nepal anyone who learns of violence or sexual abuse against a child must inform the nearest police office, and the law protects the informant (Act Relating to Children, 2018, Section 68). Which other disclosures are mandatory depends on the setting, so follow your agency's protocol and ask your supervisor when unsure. Write down that the limits were explained and agreed. {{page:ethics}} covers consent for minors and low-literacy clients. 3 · Asking what they hope for (task 5) \"What are you hoping will be different after we have met a few times? How would you know it is helping? If there are several things weighing on you, which one would you want to start with?\" Precaution: many clients arrive expecting advice, a quick fix, or a certificate. Do not promise a result; do gently say what counselling can and cannot do, and how soon change usually comes. Note the goals in their words, and any expectation you had to correct. 4 · Explaining what counselling is (task 6) \"Counselling is a conversation where we try to understand what is troubling you and find ways through it together. I will listen and ask questions; sometimes I will teach a skill, like a way to calm the body, and ask you to try it at home. Your part is to say what is true for you, even when it is hard. We will meet for about fifty minutes, once a week for now. Does that sound all right? Can you tell me in your own words what you understood?\" Precaution: the last question is the whole task. If the client cannot say it back, you explained it in your language, not theirs. Keep the con"},{"u":"first-sessions.html#first-sessions-c3","te":"The first sessions","tn":"","x":"Assessment, task by task: ask, watch, record CTEVT PSC curriculum Tasks 7 to 16 rarely happen in one sitting or in this order; the story decides the order, you make sure nothing is missed. Each row gives one opening question, one thing to watch while the person answers, and what belongs in the file. Function beats symptom lists: what can this person no longer do? The suicide row is not optional for anyone. Task Ask Watch Record 7 · Psychosocial problems \"Can you tell me what has been troubling you lately? When is it worst?\" Four dimensions: feelings, behaviour, relationships, thinking. How often, how strong, what sets it off Symptoms in the client's words, patterns and triggers, any screening score (a screen is not a diagnosis) 8 · Daily functioning \"Walk me through a usual day, from waking up.\" Self-care, sleep, meals, work or school, chores, time with people, what has dropped away since this began A short line per area, with the client's own 0 to 10 rating of how they are managing 9 · Family environment \"Who do you live with? When something goes wrong, who do you turn to at home?\" Tone and body when each person is named; who decides, who provides, who is feared. Family is not automatically support Household, roles, atmosphere, a genogram (family map) if you drew one; strengths as well as conflicts 10 · Work or school \"How are things where you work or study? How do you get on with the people there?\" Load, fairness of rules, bullying or harassment, someone there who is kind Stressors and protective people; attendance or performance changes 11 · Childhood history \"What was it like growing up? Who were you closest to?\" Attachment and losses, school, friendships, big events (a death, migration, a disaster, abuse). Go gently and stop where the client stops A brief timeline of significant events; strengths from childhood too 12 · Decision-making \"Tell me about a recent decision. How did you go about it?\" Impulsive, avoidant, or balanced; who else is involved. In many families big decisions are collective, which is a style, not a deficit Style, influences, one example in the client's words 13 · Suicidal thoughts \"Sometimes when people carry this much pain, thoughts of death or of ending their life come. Has that happened to you?\" Stay calm. If yes: plan, means, timing, past attempts, what keeps them here. Vague hints are followed up, never ignored. Never promise to keep this secret Exactly what was said, the risk level, and every action taken. The full ladder is on {{page:suicide}} 14 · Coping \"When something upsetting happens, what do you usually do?\" Problem-focused or feeling-focused; alcohol, withdrawal, aggression, self-harm. Some harmful coping began as survival Stressors, strategies, whether each helps or harms 15 · Social support \"Who is there for you when things get hard? Anyone you could call at night?\" Size and quality of the circle; emotional, practical and informational help; community groups. Draw the me-map together The map, the gaps, an"},{"u":"tools.html","te":"Tools & techniques","tn":"","x":"Alternative tools & techniques Words are not the only door. For children, for low-literacy clients, and for bodies stuck in alarm, the curriculum's toolbox, play, paper, breath, story, often works before talk does."},{"u":"tools.html#tools-c1","te":"Tools & techniques","tn":"","x":"The toolbox, tool by tool CTEVT PSC curriculum Tool How it works Precautions Play, games, drawing A child's natural language; themes surface in the sand, the doll, the picture Observe, don't interrogate the drawing; keep materials local and familiar Checklists: GAD-7 (Nepali version in the CTEVT course pack), Hopkins Symptom Checklist (HSCL-25), PTSD checklist, sentence-completion, SUDS (0–10 distress scale) Screen and track change over sessions; SUDS can track distress during exposure when that helps Nepali-validated versions exist for HSCL/PTSD scales: use them; a screen is not a diagnosis Relaxation: deep breathing, box breathing, progressive muscle relaxation (PMR), safe place imagery, counting, 5-4-3-2-1 grounding Turns the fight-or-flight body map of {{page:foundation}} down by hand: long slow exhale = parasympathetic brake Trauma clients can find closed eyes or body focus triggering; offer eyes-open grounding first. A five-step sequence for a strong feeling (notice, find, name, exhale, ask) is on {{page:techniques}} Diary / journal writing Externalises the worry loop; evidence for CBT thought-work Privacy at home is real risk: a shared room means a found diary Narrative exposure / retelling The whole life story built in order, and the traumatic events within it narrated slowly and in detail until they can be told with less distress Only with training, consent, stable clients, and time to finish what you open Tree of life / river of life Draw a life as tree (roots=origins, trunk=skills, branches=hopes) or river (bends=events); strengths-based story of self Beautiful for groups and children; let meaning come from the client Me-map (social support map) · family map (genogram) Client at centre; circles for people, distance = closeness. The genogram draws the family tree with its bonds and conflicts The curriculum's own tools for assessing social support (task 15) and the family environment (task 9) Practice drill Teach a partner box breathing (4-4-4-4) and 5-4-3-2-1 grounding, take their SUDS before and after, and record it the way you would in a session note. A worked example: A counselor named Bimal meets a 10-year-old earthquake survivor who is too distressed to talk. Bimal chooses drawing over questioning because the child's natural language is play. He also selects the SUDS scale because the child can point to a number instead of naming feelings. The child's SUDS drops from 8 to 4 after ten minutes of drawing. Key points Play, drawing, breath, story and checklists reach people that words alone cannot: children, low-literacy clients, bodies stuck in alarm. Grounding (5-4-3-2-1, slow breathing, muscle relaxation) calms the body first so the mind can work. Screening tools (GAD-7, PHQ-9, HSCL-25) track change; they suggest, they never diagnose. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. SUDS is: A sleep scale A 0 to 10 distress rat"},{"u":"techniques.html","te":"Techniques step by step","tn":"","x":"Techniques step by step: ACT, behavioural activation, ERP These are what a trained counselor does with a person over several sessions, laid out in order so a student can see the shape of each. If you are here for yourself or for someone you love, the skills to try at home are the \"A skill to try\" boxes on each disorder page; this page shows what the counselor's side of the work looks like."},{"u":"techniques.html#techniques-c1","te":"Techniques step by step","tn":"","x":"ACT: make room for the feeling, move toward what matters CTEVT PSC curriculum Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), developed by Steven Hayes, starts from one idea: the problem is rarely the anxious feeling itself, it is the struggle to get rid of it. The struggle shrinks a life. ACT works through six processes, usually woven together rather than done in order: Acceptance : let the feeling be there instead of fighting it. Defusion : see a thought as words in the mind, not as a fact. Present moment : attention on what is happening now, not on what might happen. Self as context : you are the one noticing the thoughts, not the thoughts themselves. Values : what kind of person, parent, worker, friend the client wants to be. Committed action : small steps in that direction, taken with the anxiety still present. One worked illustration (fictional): a young woman who skips meetings at work because she is afraid of being judged. The class notes teach it as nine steps; a real course of sessions loops back and forth between them. Rapport and the pattern. \"I want to understand what you are going through. There is no judgement here.\" Ask what she does when the nervousness comes. The answer, \"I skip the meeting\", shows the loop: anxiety, avoid, short relief, more anxiety next time. Explain it simply. \"Anxiety is normal. The problem is not the anxiety, it is running from it.\" The picture: quicksand, the harder you struggle, the deeper you sink. Acceptance. \"Instead of fighting the anxiety, let us learn to make space for it.\" Where is it in the body? \"In my chest.\" \"Let it be there. You do not have to remove it.\" (Eyes open is fine; closed eyes are optional.) Defusion. \"Thoughts are not facts. They are words in your mind.\" The thought \"I will embarrass myself\" becomes \"I am having the thought that I will embarrass myself.\" Said aloud many times, the sentence loses its grip. Present moment. \"Let us focus on what is happening right now.\" The 5-4-3-2-1 grounding from {{page:tools}}, used in the minutes before a meeting. Values. \"What kind of person do you want to be?\" \"I want to grow in my work.\" \"Is skipping meetings helping you grow?\" Committed action. \"You can feel anxious and still act.\" A ladder of small steps: say one sentence, ask one question, share one idea. Practice between sessions. \"Practice matters. Small steps make the change.\" Attend one meeting; say the defusion sentence once a day. Review. \"It is all right if the anxiety is still there. What matters is what you do.\" The expected outcome is honest: the anxiety may remain, the behaviour changes, confidence follows. Where it is used: anxiety disorders and depression have the best evidence. For OCD, ERP remains the first-line treatment and ACT is an addition to it; for trauma and substance use it is an adjunct, with a trained provider, not a stand-alone answer."},{"u":"techniques.html#techniques-c2","te":"Techniques step by step","tn":"","x":"Behavioural activation: action before motivation CTEVT PSC curriculum Depression makes a person do less; doing less makes the mood worse; the worse mood makes them do even less. That is the spiral on {{page:depression}}. Behavioural activation breaks it at the easiest link to move: the activity. The one line to remember is action before motivation : the client acts first, and the wish to act returns later. The class notes teach nine steps. Understand the day. Sleep, meals, who they talk to, what they have stopped doing. Explain the cycle. \"When we do less we feel worse, and when we feel worse we do less. We break it with action.\" Monitor activity. For a few days the client writes what they did each hour and how they felt. Schedule activity. A daily plan of small, realistic tasks. Not perfection, movement. Activate. Do the planned thing even when it does not feel like it. This step is the therapy. Add pleasure and achievement. One thing that is enjoyable, one that gives a sense of having done something. Solve the barriers. Tiredness, fear, no support: each named and worked around. Practise between sessions. This is where the change actually happens. Review. Every small gain counted out loud. The chart is the tool. How to introduce it: \"This chart helps us see how what you do affects how you feel. Nobody is judging you; we are learning what works.\" Activities fall into four kinds: routine (bathing, eating, sleeping), pleasure (music, a film), achievement (cleaning, finishing a task), social (talking to family or friends). Time What I did Mood 0 to 10 Pleasure 0 to 10 Achievement 0 to 10 7 to 9 am Sleeping 2 1 1 9 to 11 am Phone 3 2 1 11 to 12 Bath and breakfast 4 3 4 2 to 3 pm Short walk 5 5 5 Day Planned activity Done? Mood after Monday 10-minute walk Yes 5 Tuesday Call a friend No 3 Wednesday Clean the room Yes 6 Read the first chart with the client, not to them: the walk scored higher than the phone on all three columns, and they wrote that themselves. The \"No\" on Tuesday is information, not failure; ask what got in the way."},{"u":"techniques.html#techniques-c3","te":"Techniques step by step","tn":"","x":"ERP: face the trigger, skip the ritual CTEVT PSC curriculum Exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the behavioural treatment for OCD. It rests on the loop drawn on {{page:ocd}}: trigger, obsession, anxiety, compulsion, short relief, and the loop tightened for next time. ERP breaks it at the compulsion. This is a treatment delivered by a trained person over weeks, with consent at every step; what follows is the shape of it, not a kit to run alone; the ladder below is one example a trained clinician built with one person after assessing real risk, not a template. First, three things the client hears: an intrusive thought is frightening, but it is not an action and not a fact; the ritual calms for a minute and feeds a problem that can cost skin, sleep, work and mood for years. Then a ladder. Together they list feared situations from easiest to hardest and rate each on SUDS, the 0 to 10 distress scale from {{page:tools}} (some services use 0 to 100; the idea is the same). Example for a fear of contamination: Rung Situation SUDS 1 Touch a clean table 2 2 Touch a door handle at home 4 3 Handle money 6 4 Touch a public door handle 8 5 Do not wash for 30 minutes after touching it 9 The session. Start on the lowest rung. The client touches the table and stays with the feeling: \"Stay with the anxiety. Do not wash your hands yet.\" \"Notice the thoughts without acting on them.\" The aim is not to make the anxiety fall; it is to stay long enough to learn that the feared outcome is less likely, or more bearable, than the fear predicted, and that the anxiety, whether it falls or not, is survivable. Slow breathing may steady the body, but it is not used as an escape hatch from the feeling. SUDS is noted before, during and after, so the client can see their own record over the weeks. Response prevention is the principle that makes it ERP rather than just exposure: after the trigger, no washing, no sanitiser, no avoiding, or, where the clinician judges it right, an agreed delay that is then shortened. Practice is repeated and varied; the ladder moves up when the person has learned something new about the fear and feels ready, not when boredom sets in. For the counselor: never rush; always start low; consent and readiness before every rung; support the person warmly but do not reassure them about the fear itself (\"you will be fine\" is a ritual by proxy); keep the record. Alongside, gently test the thought: \"If I do not wash I will get sick\" becomes \"Ordinary washing, before meals and after the toilet, continues; the extra washing is the OCD talking, and I can carry the not-knowing.\" Expect a stray intrusive thought to return now and then after treatment; the plan is not to go back to the ritual, and to keep the practice going."},{"u":"techniques.html#techniques-c4","te":"Techniques step by step","tn":"","x":"Life skills: a map, not a new chapter CTEVT PSC curriculum The class notes teach \"life skills counselling\" as a ten-step course; almost every step is already taught in full elsewhere on this site. The counselor's job is to find which skill is weak (\"Which situations do you feel unable to handle?\"), say plainly that skills are learned, not inborn, then teach, rehearse by role-play, set practice, and give feedback. Six areas: Emotional regulation : naming the feeling, slow breathing; the skill box below and {{page:tools}}. Communication : I-statements (\"I feel unheard when I speak\" instead of \"you never listen\"), assertiveness; {{page:skills}}. Problem solving : name it, list options, weigh them, choose one; brainstorming on {{page:skills-moving}}. Decision making : listing pros and cons and thinking through consequences (the weighing step of brainstorming on {{page:skills-moving}}); assessed as task 12 on {{page:first-sessions}}. Stress management : muscle relaxation, grounding, the ten habits; {{page:tools}} and {{page:wellbeing}}. Social skills and routine : greeting, listening, keeping a daily shape to the day; {{page:basics}}. A skill to try · three minutes Five steps through a strong feeling. Notice early : feelings climb like a traffic light, green, yellow, red; catch it at yellow, the way you catch milk before it boils over. Your body gives the signal first: faster heart, quicker breath, a tight jaw. Find it : eyes open is fine; where does it sit, shoulders, chest, jaw? Let that place soften a little. Name it : anger is often a shield; ask \"is this anger, or is there hurt, fear or shame underneath?\" A named feeling loses some of its force. Breathe out long : in through the nose, out slowly and a little longer than in; no holding, no counting contest, stop if it feels wrong. Ask what it needs : turn it into an I-message: \"I feel ___ because I needed ___; next time, could you ___?\" Use this only where it is safe to speak plainly; where there is violence or control at home, safety comes first ({{page:gbv}}). Later, if it helps, write down what happened, what you felt and what you needed, facts first, blame left out. A coping skill, not a treatment: it supports care, it does not replace it. Key points ACT does not remove the anxiety; it loosens the struggle with it and moves the person toward what they value, one small committed step at a time. Behavioural activation runs on one rule, action before motivation: the activity chart shows the client, in their own handwriting, that what they do moves their mood. ERP faces the trigger and skips the ritual; the point of staying is to learn that the feared outcome is less likely or more bearable than predicted, and that the not-knowing can be carried; not to force the anxiety down. Never reassure about the fear itself. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. In behavioural activation, whi"},{"u":"suicide.html","te":"Suicide: assessment","tn":"","x":"Suicide: assessment & response The skill that outweighs every other in this course. The curriculum makes suicidal-thought assessment a task for every client (process task 13), because the question, asked calmly and directly, saves lives and never plants the idea. If you or someone near you is at risk right now: Nepal's National Suicide Prevention Helpline 1166 · TUTH mental-health hotline 1660 012 1600 · emergency 112 / 100 . Do not leave the person alone."},{"u":"suicide.html#suicide-c1","te":"Suicide: assessment","tn":"","x":"Myths, signs, and the ladder of risk CTEVT PSC curriculum Myth Fact Asking about suicide plants the idea Asking brings relief and opens help; it never causes suicide People who talk about it don't do it Many who die gave some warning beforehand; talking about it is often a reach for help It happens without warning Warning signs usually exist: withdrawal, giving things away, farewell talk, sudden calm after despair Once suicidal, always suicidal Acute risk is usually time-limited; getting through the crisis matters enormously Only \"mental patients\" die by suicide Many deaths follow acute life crises, debt, shame, exam failure, violence, without any prior diagnosis Assess in steps, each question earning the next: ideation (\"Sometimes when people suffer this much, thoughts of death come. Has that happened to you?\") → plan (\"Have you thought how?\") → means (\"Do you have access to that?\") → timeline and intent → past attempts (the strongest single predictor) → protective factors (children, faith, who they'd miss). Risk rises sharply with a specific plan + available means + previous attempt + intoxication + recent loss or shame. Respond by level. Levels guide urgency; they do not predict, and every plan is individual. Low (ideation, no plan): safety plan, strengthen supports, follow up soon. Medium (plan, no immediate intent): safety plan + remove means + involve a trusted person + close follow-up + supervisor. High (intent, means, or recent attempt): do not leave alone, involve a safe trusted person now (family, when family is safe), refer/accompany to psychiatric care the same day . A safety plan (warning signs → my coping steps → people to call → professionals → making my home safer) is written WITH the client. Removing means, pesticides locked away, medicines held by family, is one of the most evidence-backed interventions on earth, and in Nepal pesticide storage is the single conversation that saves most lives. Confidentiality yields to life: this is the ethical exception you must explain in session one. Nepal Suicide has repeatedly been flagged as a leading cause of death among Nepali women of reproductive age (2008/09 maternal mortality study); pesticide ingestion and hanging are the most common means; police data undercounts because of stigma and the law's history. Risk clusters: young women in early marriage, GBV survivors, indebted migrant families, and the post-monsoon exam season. After any death, ask about the client's exposure: suicide risk travels through families and schools. Test yourself 1) Write the exact words you would use to ask a 45-year-old indebted farmer about suicidal thoughts. 2) He has a plan and pesticide at home but says he \"would never do it because of the children\": what level is this, and what are your next three actions? 3) Which ethical principle overrides confidentiality here, and how did you prepare the client for it in session one? The ladder of risk. Assessment climbs one rung at a time: ideation, plan, means, in"},{"u":"pfa.html","te":"PFA & emergencies","tn":"","x":"Psychological First Aid & emergencies Nepal's counselors work in a country of earthquakes, floods, landslides, fires and bus accidents. PFA is what everyone can do in the first hours and days: humane, practical support that follows three verbs and one rule: do no harm. Someone who is not responding, badly injured, or unable to care for themselves or their children needs medical help first: ambulance 102 or 112 / 100 , then the nearest health facility. Everything on this page comes after that."},{"u":"pfa.html#pfa-c1","te":"PFA & emergencies","tn":"","x":"Look · Listen · Link, and what PFA is not CTEVT PSC curriculum LOOK Check safety of the scene Who has urgent basic needs? Who shows serious distress reactions? LISTEN Approach calmly; introduce yourself Ask about needs and concerns Listen; help people feel calm: do NOT force them to talk about what happened LINK To food, shelter, water, medical care To family and loved ones (reunification) To accurate information and services PFA is not: Professional counselling \"Debriefing\", making survivors retell the trauma; evidence shows this can harm Diagnosing Something only professionals do Most acute reactions, crying, numbness, trembling, insomnia, are normal reactions to abnormal events and settle with safety, information, and connection. The ones to escalate: Not responding at all (treat this first as a possible medical emergency: check breathing, get medical help) Risk to self or others Unable to care for self or children Remember the IASC pyramid from {{page:wellbeing}}: in an emergency your biggest mental-health act may be organising the water queue so it is safe for women at night. Test yourself A landslide has taken three houses. A woman sits silent, holding a child, not answering. Script your first five sentences of PFA: then mark which verb (look/listen/link) each sentence serves, and name two things you must NOT do. Model answer: the five sentences Here is a script for the same situation: a landslide has taken three houses, and a woman sits silent with a child. Your five sentences: 1. I am here and I am not going anywhere. 2. Tell me what happened, at your pace. 3. You are safe right now. 4. Is there anyone you would like me to contact? 5. Let us get you some water and a quiet place. Each sentence maps to a verb. Sentence 1 serves listen: you approach calmly and introduce yourself. Sentence 2 serves listen: you ask about needs and concerns. Sentence 3 serves listen: you help the person feel calm. Sentence 4 serves link: you connect to family or loved ones. Sentence 5 serves link: you connect to food, water, and shelter. Two things you must not do. Do not force the story: making a survivor retell the trauma can cause harm. Do not diagnose: you are not there to name a disorder. Your role is presence, listening, and connection. Look · Listen · Link. Three verbs anyone can carry into a flood camp or a bus-crash site. The rule under them is the whole ethic of PFA: support without pushing, and never make someone retell what they are not ready to tell. Key points PFA is humane practical support in the first hours and days: Look (safety, needs), Listen (without pressing), Link (to services and people). It is not debriefing, not counselling, not diagnosis; most acute reactions are normal responses to abnormal events. Medical danger comes first; escalate anyone unresponsive, at risk to self or others, or unable to care for themselves or children. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong "},{"u":"gbv.html","te":"Gender & GBV","tn":"","x":"Gender & gender-based violence (GBV) Sex is biology; gender is the roles, expectations and power a society attaches to it. GBV is violence that flows along those power lines, and in Nepal it is one of the most common wounds a counselor will ever sit across from. If you are in danger right now: call 112 / 100 . Women's helpline 1145 (NWC \"Khabar Garaun\"); OCMC at the nearest hospital. After sexual violence, medical care helps at any time; HIV prevention works best within 72 hours and emergency contraception within 120 hours."},{"u":"gbv.html#gbv-c1","te":"Gender & GBV","tn":"","x":"Concepts, forms, and the counselor's response CTEVT PSC curriculum Core distinctions the curriculum tests: Sex vs gender : sex is biology; gender is the roles, expectations, and power a society attaches to it Gender roles (who cooks, who inherits) and gender needs (practical vs strategic) Equality (same treatment) vs equity (fair treatment that closes gaps) Sexuality as a normal dimension of the person Forms of GBV: Physical Sexual Emotional or psychological Economic (denying money, property, work) Harmful traditional practices: in Nepal: child marriage, dowry-related violence, chhaupadi (menstrual exclusion), witchcraft (\"boksi\") accusations, and trafficking Causes run through power inequality, norms that excuse violence, impunity, and alcohol as an accelerant: never as an excuse. Survivor-centred principle In practice Safety first Is it safe to go home tonight? Safe place, safe timing of sessions, danger assessment before anything else Confidentiality Records coded; information shared only with consent, except child protection, imminent danger to life, or a legal duty, and then only the minimum needed: a leak can be lethal Respect & autonomy The survivor decides: whether to report, leave, stay; your job is options, not orders Non-discrimination Belief, not blame. Never \"why didn't you leave?\", never \"what were you wearing?\" Referral, coordinated Health, as soon as possible: within 72 hours for HIV prevention (PEP), within 120 hours for emergency contraception, and care still helps at any time; police/OCMC one-stop crisis management centres, safe houses, legal aid: with consent at every step Nepal Nepal's NDHS surveys have consistently found that a large share of ever-married women report experiencing spousal violence, most never seeking help. The system pieces you must know by name: OCMC (One-stop Crisis Management Centres in hospitals), the National Women Commission's 1145 \"Khabar Garaun\" helpline, judicial committees at the local level, and safe houses run by organisations like WOREC and Saathi. Marital rape is a crime in Nepal. Couple counselling is contraindicated while violence is active: see {{page:approaches}}. Test yourself 1) Distinguish gender equality from equity with a Nepali schooling example. 2) A survivor discloses last night's beating but forbids you to tell anyone; her danger signs are high. Walk through what survivor-centred practice allows, what safety requires, and how you hold both. 3) Name the four GBV forms hidden in: \"He took her wages, mocked her in front of guests, and she sleeps in the shed during her period.\" Model answer: mapping the four forms The sentence \"He took her wages, mocked her in front of guests, and she sleeps in the shed during her period\" hides four forms of violence. Economic violence: he took her wages, denying her money and control over property. Emotional or psychological violence: mocking her in front of guests is public humiliation that strips her of dignity and standing in the community. Physica"},{"u":"hiv.html","te":"HIV & AIDS counselling","tn":"","x":"HIV & AIDS counselling HIV is now a manageable chronic condition: a person on treatment lives a near-normal lifespan, and effective treatment with a sustained undetectable viral load makes the virus sexually untransmittable (U=U). The counselor's battle is therefore mostly with stigma, disclosure, and adherence, not the virus."},{"u":"hiv.html#hiv-c1","te":"HIV & AIDS counselling","tn":"","x":"Facts, VCT, and the psychosocial load CTEVT PSC curriculum Transmission: only four routes: Unprotected sex with an infected person Infected blood (transfusion, shared needles) Mother-to-child (pregnancy, birth, breastfeeding: largely preventable with treatment) Contaminated sharp instruments Not transmitted by touch, sharing food or utensils, mosquitoes, toilets, or living together: the facts that dissolve most household stigma. HIV (the virus) is not AIDS (the late, untreated stage); with antiretroviral therapy most people never reach AIDS. VCT: voluntary counselling and testing is the curriculum's named service. Pre-test counselling: informed consent, what the test can and cannot say (window period), risk review, preparing for either result Post-test: a negative result is a prevention conversation; a positive result is delivered simply and humanely, sitting with the shock, assessing suicide risk, planning who to tell and how, and linking to ART the same week The 5 C's: consent, confidentiality, counselling, correct results, connection to care Nepal Concentrated epidemic: key populations include people who inject drugs, sex workers and their clients, men who have sex with men, and returned migrant workers and their wives: the last group is why HIV counselling belongs in every district, not just cities. ART is free at government centres. Stigma (\"HIV = immorality\") still blocks testing; your confidentiality practice is the counterweight. Ethical note from the ACA code: disclosure to an identifiable at-risk partner is a last resort, only after working with the client toward voluntary disclosure: take such cases to supervision. Model scripts: pre-test and post-test counselling A 28-year-old woman requests HIV testing. She is nervous about what the result means. Pre-test counselling: First, explain what the test is and why we are offering it, and confirm she agrees to proceed. Second, explain the window period: the test may not detect a recent infection, so we must talk about timing and risk before the result. Third, prepare her for either outcome: whatever the result, you will walk through it together and link her to the right care the same week. Post-test counselling: Deliver the result simply and directly, in private, without jargon. Sit with the shock: give her time to absorb what she has heard before moving to the next step. Assess suicide risk by asking directly whether she has thought about ending her life, and if the answer is yes, make a safety plan and refer immediately. The VCT flow. The two highlighted boxes are where the counselor lives: before the test (consent, what a result would mean) and after it (the result, plainly, with the next step in hand). Everything else is the health system's job. Key points HIV is a manageable chronic condition; treatment gives a near-normal lifespan, and a sustained undetectable viral load means no sexual transmission (U=U). Only four routes transmit it; touch, food, mosquitoes and living together do not"},{"u":"casemgmt.html","te":"Case management","tn":"","x":"Case management: centre, documents, supervision, referral Module 2 in one page. Counselling that is not documented, supervised, and connected to a referral network is a conversation, not a service."},{"u":"casemgmt.html#casemgmt-c1","te":"Case management","tn":"","x":"The four pillars CTEVT PSC curriculum 1 · The counselling setting. The room itself is an intervention: it says you are safe here . A peaceful, private, confidential room Comfortable seating at an angle, not across a desk Tissue, water, and a first-aid box Play and drawing materials for children A waiting space that protects privacy Barriers managed: noise, interruptions, smoke, alcohol 2 · Documentation. The individual file sits under a code number, never a name . Intake form Consent form (a Nepali template is in the CTEVT course pack) Session notes: presenting problem, interventions done, plan Assessment forms and checklists (Hopkins, PTSD) Case-update and progress reports Referral forms Supervision forms Client-satisfaction and termination forms The centre's register and daily record Locked storage. Notes written the same day. Facts and observations, not judgments: write every note as if the client might one day read it, because they may. 3 · Supervision. Not inspection: protection, for the client and for you. Forms: Clinical supervision with a senior (case review, blind spots) Peer supervision (colleagues learning from each other) Your own supervision of CPSWs and trained teachers/health workers Bring your hardest case Bring your suicide-risk cases Bring the case you don't want to talk about: that one first 4 · Linkage, coordination, referral. Map your district before you need it. Know your contacts by name: Psychiatrist and hospital Senior counselor or psychologist Doctor Lawyer and human-rights organisations OCMC and safe houses VCT and drug counselors Physiotherapist Rehab centres and shelters Vocational training Schools and teachers Psychosocial-trained health post workers Child protection committees Clubs and mothers' groups Traditional healers : coordinate respectfully; they see your clients first Referral is a skill: explain why, write the referral letter, take consent, give the client the information, follow up whether they arrived. Refer up always for: Psychosis High suicide risk Severe self-neglect Children in danger Anything beyond your training Composite scenario. Priya, 24, first-episode psychosis, referred by a community health post. The health post worker noticed declining self-care and heard voices two weeks ago. Reasoning: the referral is needed because psychosis is beyond the counselor's training scope and carries risk. The referral letter reads: \"Priya, 24, presents with two weeks of auditory hallucinations and declining self-care. Suspected first-episode psychosis. Urgent psychiatric assessment needed. Consent obtained from the client. Information given to the client about the referral.\" Outcome: the psychiatrist confirmed the diagnosis and started antipsychotic medication; the counselor continued supportive sessions alongside the medical team. Test yourself Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong answer is part of learning, and you can try again. 1) List the documents"},{"u":"ethics.html","te":"Ethics & self-care","tn":"","x":"Ethics, self-care & professional growth Grounded in the ACA Code of Ethics (the code in the CTEVT course pack) and the curriculum's ethics tasks. Ethics is not a list to memorise; it is how trust is manufactured, session after session."},{"u":"ethics.html#ethics-c1","te":"Ethics & self-care","tn":"","x":"The seven working principles CTEVT PSC curriculum Principle What it demands (ACA sections) The Nepali edge-case Client welfare first Dignity and welfare above everything, including the family's wishes (A.1) When parents demand \"fix him\", the client is still the young person Informed consent Explain, verbally and simply: what counselling is, risks, fees, limits; ongoing, not one-time (A.2) Low literacy → consent by clear conversation, witnessed; minors → assent + guardian consent, weighed against the young person's growing capacity, and never when the guardian is the source of harm Confidentiality & its limits Nothing shared without consent: EXCEPT serious foreseeable harm to self or others, child abuse, or court order; disclose the minimum (B.1–B.2) In a small village everyone knows your door; where you park, who greets you: all leak. Session one covers the limits Boundaries & dual relationships No sexual/romantic contact, current or recent former clients (A.5); avoid overlapping roles; gifts weighed culturally (A.10.f) Dual relationships are unavoidable in villages: manage them openly and in supervision rather than pretending they don't exist; a khada or fruit may be respect, cash is not Competence & its boundaries Practice only what you are trained for; know your referral line; monitor your effectiveness (C.2) You are the only \"mental health person\" for hours in any direction: the temptation to exceed competence is the ethical test of the job Nondiscrimination No discrimination by caste, ethnicity, religion, gender, sexuality, disability, status (C.5) Caste is the test Nepal adds: the same cup of tea, the same chair, the same touch, for every client Records & honesty Accurate, secure, code-numbered records; honest representation of your qualifications (A.1.b, C.4) \"Counselor\" ≠ \"doctor\": never let a community upgrade your title Template in the CTEVT course pack: the Nepali informed-consent form (Nepali_Psychosocial_Informed_Consent_Form) covers exactly what this row requires: voluntary participation and the right to stop, confidentiality and its legal/safety limits, written consent before sharing information, referral possibility, no guaranteed outcome, and guardian signature for minors. Practise explaining every line aloud in plain Nepali. Self-care is an ethical duty, not a luxury (ACA C.2.g: impairment). Listening to trauma wears the listener: secondary traumatic stress and burnout (exhaustion, cynicism, dread of clients) impair the service. The curriculum's professionalism list is your maintenance schedule: Regular supervision Peer support Reading and refresher training Reasonable caseload The same sleep, movement, boundaries, and connection you prescribe to clients Care-for-caregiver training Knowing when to pause practice Growth: read journals, consult seniors, join professional associations, attend ToT and higher studies, advocate for counselling services. Ethical dilemmas: argue each side 1) Your cousin's wife books a session. 2) A 15-y"},{"u":"ethics.html#ethics-c2","te":"Ethics & self-care","tn":"","x":"The APA's five principles, and a ladder for hard decisions CTEVT PSC curriculum The class notes teach the American Psychological Association code alongside the ACA's. Its five general principles are aspirations (what a good counselor reaches for); its ethical standards are enforceable rules. The seven working principles above are the standards in daily clothes. Principle In one line Where it bites in Nepal A · Beneficence and nonmaleficence Do good, and above all do no harm; look after your own burnout before it reaches the client One counselor for a whole district: rest is part of the job B · Fidelity and responsibility Be trustworthy, know your role, own your duty to the community Say clearly what you are and are not; keep the referral promise C · Integrity Honest, accurate, no deception or inflated credentials \"Counselor\" stays \"counselor\" on the signboard D · Justice Fair access and equal quality for everyone; watch your own bias Caste, language, distance from the road E · Respect for rights and dignity Privacy, self-determination, cultural identity The client decides, even when the family speaks first Confidentiality's three mandatory limits , in the APA's words: Imminent danger to self or others (the \"duty to warn\") Suspected abuse or neglect of a child, elder, or vulnerable adult A binding legal order When a dilemma has no clean answer, climb the ladder: Name the conflict precisely; code against law against client wish Map the context: culture, vulnerability, power, who else is affected Consult: the code, the law, your supervisor List the options and weigh each one's consequences Act, and write down what you decided and why Key points Client welfare first, informed consent, confidentiality with its limits, boundaries, competence, nondiscrimination, honest records: the seven working principles. Confidentiality yields only to imminent danger, child abuse, or a legal order, and then only the minimum needed. Self-care is an ethical duty: supervision, rest and boundaries keep the counselor safe for the client. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. Confidentiality may be broken when: The family asks There is serious foreseeable harm to self or others The neighbour is curious ✔ Yes. Harm risk, child abuse, and court orders are the classic limits, disclosed minimally. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. In a village where everyone knows you, dual relationships are: Forbidden, so refuse all clients Unavoidable; manage them openly and in supervision Not an ethical issue ✔ Yes. Pretending they do not exist is the real danger. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Counselor self-care is: A luxury An ethical duty, because impairment harms clients Optional after retirement ✔ Yes. ACA C.2.g: monitor yourself and pause when impaired. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section"},{"u":"more.html","te":"The rest of the map","tn":"","x":"What we haven't touched yet: one line each So the map has no blank spaces: the remaining DSM-5 categories, one honest sentence each. Any of these can become a full chapter later. Category Flagship disorders One-line handle Bipolar & related Bipolar I · II · cyclothymic Mania (≥1 wk) defines Bipolar I, with or without depression; hypomania (≥4 d) plus major depression, never mania, defines Bipolar II; see the boundary note on the Depression page Dissociative Dissociative identity · dissociative amnesia · depersonalisation/derealisation Disrupted integration of memory, identity, perception: strongly trauma-linked Personality disorders Cluster A (paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal) · B (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic) · C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive) Enduring, inflexible patterns of inner experience and behaviour from early adulthood; BPD (instability of relationships, self-image, affect; abandonment fear; self-harm risk) is the one the Mental Health-3 class notes name Neurocognitive Delirium · major/mild neurocognitive disorder (Alzheimer's, vascular…) Acquired decline in cognition; delirium is acute, fluctuating, medical: an emergency, not counseling material Gender dysphoria Gender dysphoria Distress from incongruence between experienced and assigned gender: the dysphoria, not the identity, is the clinical focus Elimination Enuresis · encopresis Bed-wetting past age 5 / soiling past 4: rule out medical causes, never punish Sexual dysfunctions Desire, arousal, orgasm, pain disorders ≥6 months, distressing; rarely volunteered in Nepal: ask privately Paraphilic disorders Pedophilic, exhibitionistic, voyeuristic… Atypical arousal is a disorder only when it causes distress/impairment or involves non-consent Key points DSM-5 has about twenty chapters; this atlas covers ten in depth and the rest in one honest sentence each. Bipolar I is defined by mania; bipolar II by hypomania plus depression; delirium is a medical emergency, not a counselling case. Knowing a category exists is enough to refer well; any of these can become a full chapter later. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you can try again. A full manic episode (1 week or more) means: Bipolar I Bipolar II Cyclothymia ✔ Yes. Hypomania (4 days, milder) plus depression makes Bipolar II. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Borderline personality disorder's hallmark is: Perfect stability Instability of relationships, self-image and emotion, with abandonment fear Hallucinations ✔ Yes. Named in your Mental Health-3 notes; self-harm risk demands care. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Delirium is best treated as: A counselling case An acute medical emergency Normal ageing ✔ Yes. Acute, fluctuating confusion has a medical cause; refer fast. Not quite; have another look. The answer is in this section. Learn more Pick one, no"},{"u":"nepal.html","te":"Culture & the Nepal panorama","tn":"","x":"The Nepal panorama The cross-cutting Nepali material every chapter leaned on, gathered in one place: the ethnopsychology, the idioms of distress, and the history of mental health care. The Nepali map of the person (after Kohrt & Harper's ethnopsychology work). A skilled counselor speaks to the man (heart-mind), \"man ko kura\", because problems of the man are shareable, while \"dimaag kharab\" invites stigma. The dotted ring is saato and ijjat: fright and honour, both of which the family will be managing alongside the symptom. Idioms of distress: hear these, think of these Man dukhne / man bhari heart-mind aching / heavy → depression screen Man aattine heart-mind panicking → anxiety, panic Jham-jham tingling-numbness in limbs → anxiety, hyperventilation, somatic presentation Jiu galne / kamjori body wilting / weakness → depression, GAD, anaemia work-up Saato jaane soul flees after fright → acute stress, trauma; healer-treated Chhopne / lagne possession-like state / spirit affliction → conversion, dissociation, or culturally normal trance Nindra nalagne no sleep → the universal doorway complaint Mental health care in Nepal: the timeline (from MH-3) Pre-1960s : distress read through karma and spirits; dhami-jhankri and elders as first help 1961–62 : first psychiatric OPD, Bir Hospital (Dr. Bishnu Prasad Sharma) 1984–85 : Mental Hospital, Lagankhel, Patan 1996 : first National Mental Health Policy (primary-care integration) 2020 : National Mental Health Strategy & Action Plan: community psychosocial workers, task-sharing; the policy ground your own profession stands on"},{"u":"nepal.html#nepal-c1","te":"Culture & the Nepal panorama","tn":"","x":"Culture-bound syndromes (DSM-5: \"cultural concepts of distress\") & healing systems CTEVT PSC curriculum The curriculum names three: dhat, possession, soul loss . A culture-bound syndrome is a pattern of distress recognised and named by a culture, coherent inside its own explanatory world. Syndrome Where Presentation Clinical neighbour Dhat South Asia Anxiety, weakness, guilt attributed to semen loss Anxiety · depressive · somatic symptoms Possession / chhopne Nepal, South Asia Trance, altered voice, another's identity speaks; often adolescent girls, can spread in groups Conversion · dissociation, or culturally normal ritual trance (a jhankri's trance is his job, not a disorder) Saato jaanu (soul loss) Nepal After fright: weakness, appetite loss, withdrawal; healed by saato bolaune (calling the spirit) Acute stress reaction · adjustment Khyâl cap (\"wind attack\") Cambodia Panic attributed to wind rising in the body Panic attack Ataque de nervios Latin America Shouting, crying, trembling after family stress Panic-like episode Taijin kyofusho Japan, Korea Fear of offending others with one's body or gaze Social anxiety variant Healing systems your clients use, usually before you: Dhami-jhankri : shamanic diagnosis and ritual Jharphuk : sweeping or blowing away affliction Vaidya and Ayurveda Lamas and monasteries , temple vows and pujas The evidence-based stance is collaboration: healers refer what they cannot heal when they are respected, and families accept counselling more easily when it does not demand they abandon their world. The counselor's line: work alongside any practice that is not harmful; act on harmful ones (beating \"spirits\" out, chaining, denial of urgent medical care): through relationship where possible, through protection systems where necessary. Composite scenario. A client says, \"man dukhne chha\" (the heart-mind aches). The counselor opens with this local term rather than labeling it depression Reasoning: using the client's own words builds trust and honors the cultural framework Outcome: the client shares more about sleep loss and appetite, and the counselor can screen accurately without imposing a Western category first The standing rule: never invent Nepali prevalence figures. For OCD, eating disorders, and sleep disorders no national figure exists: say so. Where Nepali evidence does exist (post-earthquake distress studies, the maternal-mortality suicide finding, the National Mental Health Survey 2020), cite it by name and year. Key points Nepali distress speaks through the 'man' (heart-mind), the body, and spirit idioms: 'man dukhcha', 'sato gayo', 'chhopne'. Traditional healers are the first contact for most families; collaborate with them, do not compete. Formal care is young (Bir OPD 1961, Lagankhel 1985, policy 1996, strategy 2020) and thin outside cities; task-sharing fills the gap. Quick check Three friendly questions to settle what you just read. This is reassurance, not a test: a wrong tap is part of learning, and you c"},{"u":"crosswalk.html","te":"For PSC students","tn":"","x":"For CTEVT PSC students: how this atlas maps to your course The CTEVT Psychosocial Counselor course has four modules. This page shows which pages of the atlas teach which part, so you always know where you are in the syllabus. Everyone else can safely skip it. Where you are What the course calls it Status Here on the atlas Module 1 · Psychosocial Intervention , 165 h · 100 marks Wellbeing, Intervention & CBI (20 h) partly {{page:wellbeing}} Mental Health (34 h) covered here {{page:basics}} · {{page:anxiety}} · {{page:depression}} · {{page:somatic}} · {{page:psychosis}} · {{page:child}} · {{page:nepal}} (Gender,) Culture & Counselling (24 h) partly {{page:nepal}} Human Development & Behavior (18 h) covered here {{page:development}} Special Issues (69 h) partly {{page:hiv}} · {{page:substance}} · {{page:gbv}} · {{page:suicide}} · {{page:somatic}} · {{page:trauma}} · {{page:pfa}} Module 2 · Case Management , 60 h · 150 marks Managing the Counselling Center (12 h) partly {{page:casemgmt}} · {{page:process}} Documentation (12 h) partly {{page:casemgmt}} · {{page:first-sessions}} Supervision (18 h) partly {{page:casemgmt}} · {{page:ethics}} Linkage, Coordination & Referrals (18 h) partly {{page:casemgmt}} · {{page:nepal}} · {{page:gbv}} · {{page:hiv}} Module 3 · Counseling Skill and Process , 322 h · 200 marks Approaches (30 h) covered here {{page:approaches}} · {{page:techniques}} Types & Forms of Counselling (43 h) partly {{page:approaches}} Basics of Counselling (14 h) partly {{page:approaches}} · {{page:basics}} · {{page:nepal}} · {{page:ethics}} Communication Skills (99 h) covered here {{page:skills}} · {{page:skills-listening}} · {{page:skills-moving}} Counseling Process (82 h) covered here {{page:process}} · {{page:first-sessions}} Alternative Tools & Techniques (54 h) partly {{page:tools}} · {{page:techniques}} Module 4 · Capacity Building , 73 h · 50 marks Facilitation Skill (59 h) not yet on the site nothing yet Professionalism Development (16 h) partly {{page:ethics}} How to read the statuses: \"covered here\" means the atlas teaches most of that sub-module at curriculum depth. \"Partly\" means the topic is named here, not taught in full; take those rows to your trainer to go deeper. \"Not yet on the site\" means nothing is written yet. Statuses are honest on purpose, so you know what to ask for. A few pages sit beyond the exam syllabus (OCD, eating, sleep, the deep dives) and carry the \"beyond curriculum\" tag on their own pages. The course in numbers 780 hours over six months, 620 in-house plus 160 OJT. 600 marks : 100 theory, 500 practical. Pass at a 60% average ; 90% attendance required. Five of every six marks are practical, so practising the skills matters. Module Hours Marks 1 · Psychosocial Intervention 165 100 2 · Case Management 60 150 3 · Counseling Skill and Process 322 200 4 · Capacity Building 73 50 OJT (practical placement) 160 100 The curriculum PDF numbers its modules two ways in different places. This site follows the course-struct"},{"u":"glossary.html","te":"Glossary","tn":"","x":"Glossary: the recurring clinical words Terms that appear again and again across the atlas, each in one plain sentence. The English term is kept because it is what you will hear in hospitals, referral letters and exams; the Nepali column gives the meaning, not a coined translation."},{"u":"glossary.html#glossary-c1","te":"Glossary","tn":"","x":"A to W Beyond curriculum Term Plain meaning Where it lives Anhedonia Losing the ability to enjoy things that used to give pleasure Depressive disorders Avolition Loss of drive to start or finish everyday tasks Psychosis Behavioural activation Treating low mood by scheduling small activities first and letting the mood follow, rather than waiting to feel like it Techniques step by step Biopsychosocial Illness understood as biology, mind and social world acting together Mental health basics Cognitive defusion Stepping back from a thought so it is seen as words in the mind, not a fact to obey (\"I am having the thought that…\") Techniques step by step Cognitive distortion A habitual, unfair way of thinking, such as catastrophising Mental health basics Compulsion A repeated act or ritual done to relieve an obsession OCD & related Conditions of worth Rules learned early about when one deserves love (\"only if I come first\"); Rogers' term for what blocks growth Approaches & forms Conversion Real body symptoms (fits, paralysis) without a matching disease Conversion & somatic Delusion A fixed false belief that evidence cannot shift Psychosis Depersonalisation Feeling detached from one's own body or self, as if watching oneself Anxiety disorders Derealisation The world around feels unreal, foggy or dream-like Anxiety disorders Diathesis-stress A built-in vulnerability that becomes illness when stress hits Mental health basics Dissociation Mind and body disconnecting under stress: blanking out, losing time Trauma & stressor Exposure Facing a feared thing in small planned steps until fear fades Fear: the foundation Formulation (4-P) Predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors turned into a plan The counselling process Grounding Simple senses-and-breath techniques that calm the body in minutes Tools & techniques Hallucination Hearing, seeing or feeling something that is not there Psychosis Hyperarousal The body stuck on high alert: jumpy, sleepless, irritable Trauma & stressor Hypomania A milder, shorter high mood with less sleep and more energy (bipolar II) The rest of the map Obsession An unwanted thought or image that keeps intruding OCD & related Paralanguage Everything in the voice except the words: tone, pace, volume, pauses Communication skills Peripartum During pregnancy or in the weeks after birth Depressive disorders Post-ictal The confused, drowsy state after an epileptic seizure Conversion & somatic Proxemics How people use distance and seating; in counselling, sitting at an angle with no desk between Communication skills Psychoeducation Explaining a condition to a person and family in plain words Anxiety disorders Psychosis A break with shared reality: delusions, hallucinations, disorganisation Psychosis Reinforcement Anything that makes a behaviour more likely to happen again Fear: the foundation Response prevention In OCD work, staying with the trigger without doing the ritual, so the person tests the fear's prediction and lea"}];